(LeBron screaming)

00:03:03.126 --> 00:03:05.793
(intense music)

00:03:26.890 --> 00:03:29.040
- Hi everyone, and
welcome to the Academy's

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:31.440
Visiting Artists Film Series,

00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:33.710
FilmAid Edition for Film Composers.

00:03:33.710 --> 00:03:35.010
I'm Taura Stinson,

00:03:35.010 --> 00:03:38.130
and today I have the
pleasure of interviewing

00:03:38.130 --> 00:03:40.252
the amazing Kris Bowers.

00:03:40.252 --> 00:03:41.573
- Hi Taura, good to see you.

00:03:41.573 --> 00:03:43.150
- Good to see you, Kris.

00:03:43.150 --> 00:03:44.370
- Yeah, thanks so much.

00:03:44.370 --> 00:03:45.230
- Oh, thank you.

00:03:45.230 --> 00:03:47.830
This is amazing moment.

00:03:47.830 --> 00:03:49.120
I'm happy to...

00:03:49.120 --> 00:03:52.050
I have so many questions for
you, but so does everyone else,

00:03:52.050 --> 00:03:53.190
so I have to get in line?

00:03:53.190 --> 00:03:54.660
(both laughing)

00:03:54.660 --> 00:03:56.850
I'm just gonna jump right in.

00:03:56.850 --> 00:03:59.040
I know that, like a
dream of mine growing up,

00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:00.110
was to go to Juilliard,

00:04:00.110 --> 00:04:01.768
but you actually went there.

00:04:01.768 --> 00:04:03.500
(both laughing)

00:04:03.500 --> 00:04:05.010
And that's amazing.

00:04:05.010 --> 00:04:06.250
I know...

00:04:06.250 --> 00:04:08.740
I just wanna know a little bit about that.

00:04:08.740 --> 00:04:10.840
You don't just wake up and
decide to go to Juilliard.

00:04:10.840 --> 00:04:12.110
So this must have been a dream

00:04:12.110 --> 00:04:15.990
that was brewing inside of you for years.

00:04:15.990 --> 00:04:18.240
Can you tell us a little bit about that?

00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:20.083
- Yeah, you know, it's interesting.

00:04:20.083 --> 00:04:23.670
I didn't actually decide that
I wanted to go to Juilliard

00:04:23.670 --> 00:04:28.490
until I had gotten past the
first round of auditions.

00:04:28.490 --> 00:04:30.450
My parents decided before I was born,

00:04:30.450 --> 00:04:31.500
they wanted me to play piano,

00:04:31.500 --> 00:04:32.700
and they-
- Oh wow.

00:04:32.700 --> 00:04:35.760
- They really did a lot to
make sure that they put me

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:38.207
in the best environments
for music education,

00:04:38.207 --> 00:04:40.860
and they drove all over LA
to find the best teachers

00:04:40.860 --> 00:04:43.070
and all this stuff, and I was

00:04:43.070 --> 00:04:45.774
studying piano from the
time I was four up until

00:04:45.774 --> 00:04:47.950
all through high school.

00:04:47.950 --> 00:04:50.050
And so I knew I wanted
to go to a conservatory,

00:04:50.050 --> 00:04:52.840
but when I was a junior, I
had gotten a full scholarship

00:04:52.840 --> 00:04:55.270
to Berkeley College of Music in Boston,

00:04:55.270 --> 00:04:56.937
and I told my parents,
"That's where I'm going,

00:04:56.937 --> 00:04:59.220
"and there's no conversation
about it," essentially.

00:04:59.220 --> 00:05:03.970
And they were concerned
that it didn't feel like

00:05:03.970 --> 00:05:05.730
the best structure in their mind

00:05:05.730 --> 00:05:07.230
as far as a school environment,

00:05:07.230 --> 00:05:10.860
and they wanted me to
consider maybe going to USC

00:05:10.860 --> 00:05:15.160
or going to another school
where I could major in music,

00:05:15.160 --> 00:05:16.980
but I could maybe minor in something else

00:05:16.980 --> 00:05:19.840
or get like a general
education at the same time.

00:05:19.840 --> 00:05:21.100
And the idea of the conservatory

00:05:21.100 --> 00:05:22.860
was kind of making them nervous.

00:05:22.860 --> 00:05:25.450
And it was actually a teacher of mine

00:05:25.450 --> 00:05:28.440
and a mentor named Eric Reed,
this amazing jazz pianist,

00:05:28.440 --> 00:05:31.070
who suggested to me and to my parents

00:05:31.070 --> 00:05:32.230
that I should look at Juilliard,

00:05:32.230 --> 00:05:34.100
and that it was a really great program,

00:05:34.100 --> 00:05:35.560
and it also would provide that structure

00:05:35.560 --> 00:05:37.140
that my parents were looking for.

00:05:37.140 --> 00:05:39.590
And so I did the first
audition and I was kind of

00:05:40.440 --> 00:05:42.170
not really that interested, to be honest,

00:05:42.170 --> 00:05:44.520
and then I hadn't really
looked at the program.

00:05:44.520 --> 00:05:47.601
And after I got through
that first audition process,

00:05:47.601 --> 00:05:50.740
I just looked deeper into the
program and realized that,

00:05:50.740 --> 00:05:53.810
oh, if I get in I'm one
of four jazz pianists

00:05:53.810 --> 00:05:54.643
in the program.

00:05:54.643 --> 00:05:58.070
And so the chances of me
studying with Kenny Barron

00:05:58.070 --> 00:06:00.010
or Eric Reed or whomever
I wanna study with

00:06:00.010 --> 00:06:01.690
is much higher than if I go to Berkeley,

00:06:01.690 --> 00:06:03.890
and I'm one of hundreds of pianists.

00:06:03.890 --> 00:06:05.860
And if I wanna study
with my favorite teacher,

00:06:05.860 --> 00:06:08.060
that might take me a
few years to get there.

00:06:08.060 --> 00:06:11.520
So the idea of how small
that environment was

00:06:11.520 --> 00:06:12.680
and how familiar it was,

00:06:12.680 --> 00:06:15.030
especially because they had a process

00:06:15.030 --> 00:06:18.450
where they only accepted what
they lost the previous year.

00:06:18.450 --> 00:06:22.820
And so it was a really
big mix of years and ages.

00:06:22.820 --> 00:06:24.860
So my freshman year when I got there,

00:06:24.860 --> 00:06:26.980
the ensemble that I was in, it was like,

00:06:26.980 --> 00:06:29.060
me as a freshman and
then a couple of seniors

00:06:29.060 --> 00:06:30.220
and a couple of masters students.

00:06:30.220 --> 00:06:33.070
So it immediately puts
you in this position

00:06:33.070 --> 00:06:34.760
where you have to
operate on the same level

00:06:34.760 --> 00:06:37.100
as all these other players
who are older than you,

00:06:37.100 --> 00:06:38.140
or more experienced,

00:06:38.140 --> 00:06:40.330
and so once I understood
the program a bit more,

00:06:40.330 --> 00:06:43.310
then that became like the
only school I wanted to go to.

00:06:43.310 --> 00:06:45.640
And so I just really lucked out that

00:06:45.640 --> 00:06:47.468
in that second part of
the audition process

00:06:47.468 --> 00:06:49.260
I ended up getting in.

00:06:49.260 --> 00:06:50.950
- Wow, that is incredible.

00:06:50.950 --> 00:06:52.360
Way to go, mom and dad.

00:06:52.360 --> 00:06:53.883
- Yeah.
- For that foresight.

00:06:54.880 --> 00:06:58.520
So, you studied jazz there?

00:06:58.520 --> 00:06:59.985
- Yeah, yeah.
- So,

00:06:59.985 --> 00:07:04.353
I wanna know about the transition
from jazz to film scoring.

00:07:05.860 --> 00:07:07.520
Did you always wanna score film?

00:07:07.520 --> 00:07:10.810
Or did you wanna be a big jazz artist?

00:07:10.810 --> 00:07:11.820
Or what was your dream?

00:07:11.820 --> 00:07:13.970
And are you living your dream?

00:07:13.970 --> 00:07:16.370
There's so many parts of this question.

00:07:16.370 --> 00:07:19.610
- Yeah, my parents, like I mentioned,

00:07:19.610 --> 00:07:20.920
as far as their plan for me,

00:07:20.920 --> 00:07:24.640
were very serious about me
also having a plan for myself.

00:07:24.640 --> 00:07:27.010
They asked me when I was like,

00:07:27.010 --> 00:07:28.917
when I was a freshman in
high school, my dad was like,

00:07:28.917 --> 00:07:30.387
"Okay, well, what's the plan for college?

00:07:30.387 --> 00:07:31.807
"What do you think you wanna do?

00:07:31.807 --> 00:07:33.950
"And what's the trajectory?"

00:07:33.950 --> 00:07:35.090
Essentially.

00:07:35.090 --> 00:07:36.660
And at that time,

00:07:36.660 --> 00:07:38.580
I had really fallen in
love with film music,

00:07:38.580 --> 00:07:41.427
just because my dad was
a writer for film and TV,

00:07:41.427 --> 00:07:43.963
and so movies were just like,

00:07:45.170 --> 00:07:46.870
something that we gathered around.

00:07:46.870 --> 00:07:49.040
He always wanted to show
us his favorite films.

00:07:49.040 --> 00:07:50.500
Anytime a new movie came out,

00:07:50.500 --> 00:07:51.860
we were immediately there at the theater

00:07:51.860 --> 00:07:53.630
to see that movie that weekend.

00:07:53.630 --> 00:07:55.360
And up until that point,

00:07:55.360 --> 00:07:58.710
piano had been this medium
of expression for me.

00:07:58.710 --> 00:08:01.760
Like once I started learning
jazz and improvisation,

00:08:01.760 --> 00:08:04.160
the thing that made me want
to continue to play piano

00:08:04.160 --> 00:08:06.430
and make it my thing was the fact that

00:08:06.430 --> 00:08:09.000
it felt like a safe space
for me to express emotion.

00:08:09.000 --> 00:08:12.540
And so once I recognized
that there was a job

00:08:12.540 --> 00:08:17.380
where I literally could
translate emotion into music,

00:08:17.380 --> 00:08:19.100
realizing that I could listen to the score

00:08:19.100 --> 00:08:22.860
from "Jurassic Park" and feel
the same level of adventure

00:08:22.860 --> 00:08:26.540
and excitement that I did
when I was watching the movie,

00:08:26.540 --> 00:08:27.800
I just told my parents,

00:08:27.800 --> 00:08:29.300
when my dad had that conversation with me,

00:08:29.300 --> 00:08:31.397
when I was like 12, I was like,

00:08:31.397 --> 00:08:33.293
"I wanna go to college for jazz piano,

00:08:33.293 --> 00:08:35.287
"and then I wanna tour with
my favorite jazz artist.

00:08:35.287 --> 00:08:36.817
"Then I wanna tour with my own jazz band,

00:08:36.817 --> 00:08:38.910
"and then transition into film scoring."

00:08:38.910 --> 00:08:41.650
And I honestly, like when
I told him that plan,

00:08:41.650 --> 00:08:44.580
I was like, yeah, so maybe
by the time I'm like 40,

00:08:44.580 --> 00:08:46.611
I'll be getting into
film scoring essentially.

00:08:46.611 --> 00:08:47.950
(both laughing)

00:08:47.950 --> 00:08:51.880
It was just a combination
of the fact that I,

00:08:51.880 --> 00:08:54.300
one, I think the touring life was just...

00:08:54.300 --> 00:08:56.097
I mean, you know how it is.

00:08:56.097 --> 00:08:57.270
(voices muffling)

00:08:57.270 --> 00:08:59.370
Yeah, like the first few years I was like,

00:08:59.370 --> 00:09:03.173
I can't do this for like 15, 20 years.

00:09:03.173 --> 00:09:05.360
And also what was interesting

00:09:05.360 --> 00:09:07.580
is that some of my first projects came

00:09:07.580 --> 00:09:09.910
from people who heard me talk about that

00:09:09.910 --> 00:09:10.920
from that young age.

00:09:10.920 --> 00:09:14.630
Like I scored a documentary
about Kobe Bryant

00:09:14.630 --> 00:09:16.196
that was one of my first projects,

00:09:16.196 --> 00:09:18.530
and that came because a friend of mine

00:09:18.530 --> 00:09:21.710
who I was in a high school
all star jazz band with

00:09:22.610 --> 00:09:25.129
came to me at a show
here in LA with my band,

00:09:25.129 --> 00:09:27.350
and we hadn't seen each
other since high school,

00:09:27.350 --> 00:09:29.757
and he was like, "Man,
I remember when we were

00:09:29.757 --> 00:09:30.957
"in that band in high school

00:09:30.957 --> 00:09:33.367
"you mentioned wanting
to be a film composer,

00:09:33.367 --> 00:09:35.667
"and I'm producing this
documentary about Kobe,

00:09:35.667 --> 00:09:36.677
"and I love your music,

00:09:36.677 --> 00:09:38.990
"and I feel like you'd be
great to compose for it."

00:09:38.990 --> 00:09:39.960
And-
- Wow.

00:09:39.960 --> 00:09:42.080
- So I feel like a lot of
those first opportunities

00:09:42.080 --> 00:09:46.020
really just came because I
talked about that plan so much

00:09:46.020 --> 00:09:49.073
that as soon as somebody
thought, oh, I need a composer,

00:09:50.083 --> 00:09:51.560
and recognized that I wanted to do that,

00:09:51.560 --> 00:09:54.640
they just kind of helped me

00:09:54.640 --> 00:09:56.540
with that transition, essentially.

00:09:56.540 --> 00:09:57.910
One other the funny thing,

00:09:57.910 --> 00:09:59.530
which is like a slight connection for us,

00:09:59.530 --> 00:10:02.510
is I know you obviously were nominated

00:10:02.510 --> 00:10:05.160
for "Mudbound" and with Dee Reese,

00:10:05.160 --> 00:10:06.840
the first time that that happened,

00:10:06.840 --> 00:10:08.547
where somebody was like,
"Oh, you wanna be a composer,

00:10:08.547 --> 00:10:12.030
"help me out," was Dee
Reese's first short film,

00:10:12.030 --> 00:10:15.060
or a short film of hers
when I was in high school.

00:10:15.060 --> 00:10:15.893
- Oh, wow.

00:10:15.893 --> 00:10:16.940
- I didn't even know how I met her,

00:10:16.940 --> 00:10:19.623
but she asked me if I
would score that film.

00:10:19.623 --> 00:10:21.250
And I was like, "Yeah, I
want to," and I got home,

00:10:21.250 --> 00:10:22.640
and I was like, I have no idea what to do.

00:10:22.640 --> 00:10:25.410
I put the VHS in my VHS
player, and I was like,

00:10:25.410 --> 00:10:27.190
what do I do now?
(both laughing)

00:10:27.190 --> 00:10:28.023
- Wow.

00:10:28.023 --> 00:10:29.560
- I told her like, "I'm sorry,
I can't score your film."

00:10:29.560 --> 00:10:32.688
And then she became Dee
Reese, and I was like, oh man.

00:10:32.688 --> 00:10:33.640
(voice muffling)

00:10:33.640 --> 00:10:35.280
- Dee Reese, that's amazing.

00:10:35.280 --> 00:10:36.352
And you know what,

00:10:36.352 --> 00:10:37.580
we're gonna have to shorten

00:10:37.580 --> 00:10:39.810
that six degrees of separation

00:10:39.810 --> 00:10:41.639
and work together one of these days.

00:10:41.639 --> 00:10:42.970
- Yeah, for sure.
- That's amazing.

00:10:42.970 --> 00:10:45.910
Yeah, and that year I
remember meeting Kobe,

00:10:45.910 --> 00:10:47.799
and just like, huh.

00:10:47.799 --> 00:10:48.960
(voice muffling)

00:10:48.960 --> 00:10:49.970
Icon.

00:10:49.970 --> 00:10:51.783
How was it working with him?

00:10:51.783 --> 00:10:52.940
- Man, yeah, it was amazing.

00:10:52.940 --> 00:10:57.940
I mean, he actually has had a
huge impact on my mentality,

00:10:58.470 --> 00:11:00.450
my life, my approach to film scoring.

00:11:00.450 --> 00:11:02.358
So I worked with him on that documentary,

00:11:02.358 --> 00:11:05.490
but also, one, with that documentary,

00:11:05.490 --> 00:11:06.627
what was interesting is that

00:11:06.627 --> 00:11:09.726
when I was brought on
the director was like,

00:11:09.726 --> 00:11:11.660
we're pretty much done.

00:11:11.660 --> 00:11:12.493
We have a rough cut.

00:11:12.493 --> 00:11:13.710
We're gonna show it to Kobe.

00:11:13.710 --> 00:11:15.480
And then we'll just
kinda edit it together,

00:11:15.480 --> 00:11:17.540
and then make the final edits,

00:11:17.540 --> 00:11:19.830
and then add the music, and we'll be done.

00:11:19.830 --> 00:11:22.577
And they showed it to
Kobe, and Kobe was like,

00:11:22.577 --> 00:11:23.930
"This is unacceptable,"

00:11:23.930 --> 00:11:27.230
and made them start over
from scratch, essentially.

00:11:27.230 --> 00:11:29.410
And I was already-
- He has that...

00:11:29.410 --> 00:11:31.617
- Yeah, exactly that Mamba mentality.

00:11:32.597 --> 00:11:34.110
And I was already on the team,

00:11:34.110 --> 00:11:36.550
and so he just included me in the process

00:11:36.550 --> 00:11:39.790
of building this film from the ground up.

00:11:39.790 --> 00:11:42.140
And so I saw them put it together

00:11:42.140 --> 00:11:44.020
and saw him kind of guiding that process

00:11:44.020 --> 00:11:45.540
and learning about film along the way,

00:11:45.540 --> 00:11:48.840
and so I was learning a lot
at the same time watching him,

00:11:48.840 --> 00:11:50.550
but he would often...

00:11:50.550 --> 00:11:52.650
One of the things he told
me that still sticks with me

00:11:52.650 --> 00:11:54.690
in our first meeting was that,

00:11:54.690 --> 00:11:55.987
he was like, "Watch this film

00:11:55.987 --> 00:11:58.757
"and look at all these
experiences in my life,

00:11:58.757 --> 00:12:02.877
"but think about emotional
moments in your own life

00:12:02.877 --> 00:12:05.537
"that are parallel to these things for me

00:12:05.537 --> 00:12:06.827
"and write from that place,

00:12:06.827 --> 00:12:08.718
"from your personal, emotional place,

00:12:08.718 --> 00:12:10.920
"and it's gonna be great, and..."

00:12:10.920 --> 00:12:12.820
- Wait, that was his advice to you?

00:12:12.820 --> 00:12:14.020
- Yeah, yeah.

00:12:14.020 --> 00:12:15.710
- Wow, like, wow.

00:12:15.710 --> 00:12:18.037
- Yeah.
- That's incredible.

00:12:18.037 --> 00:12:19.670
- And he was always pushing me

00:12:19.670 --> 00:12:21.430
to write the best music possible,

00:12:21.430 --> 00:12:23.330
and then I ended up working on,

00:12:23.330 --> 00:12:25.920
he wrote these children's books,

00:12:25.920 --> 00:12:27.760
and I scored the audiobooks for those,

00:12:27.760 --> 00:12:31.900
and working on his documentary
about his final season.

00:12:31.900 --> 00:12:33.730
So I actually had a conversation with him

00:12:33.730 --> 00:12:35.060
literally a week before he passed,

00:12:35.060 --> 00:12:37.651
'cause we were still workin'
together on projects.

00:12:37.651 --> 00:12:40.670
But he was constantly
helping me think about

00:12:40.670 --> 00:12:44.410
how I could approach my craft
in the best way possible

00:12:44.410 --> 00:12:45.250
and from different ways.

00:12:45.250 --> 00:12:49.070
And it also helps that he
is a huge John Williams fan,

00:12:49.070 --> 00:12:51.030
and actually used to call John Williams

00:12:51.030 --> 00:12:52.190
a couple times a year,

00:12:52.190 --> 00:12:53.820
like a few times a year, they would just-

00:12:53.820 --> 00:12:55.010
- Wow.
Chat about process.

00:12:55.010 --> 00:12:57.227
- So yeah.
- Oh, just icon to icon,

00:12:57.227 --> 00:12:58.693
"Hello."
- Right.

00:12:58.693 --> 00:12:59.801
(both laughing)

00:12:59.801 --> 00:13:00.979
It's like, I wish I could (voice muffling)

00:13:00.979 --> 00:13:02.290
just listening to those conversations.

00:13:02.290 --> 00:13:04.983
- Wow, that is incredible.

00:13:06.000 --> 00:13:11.000
So Kobe also had this
huge impact on children

00:13:11.850 --> 00:13:15.140
and like a service, I feel like,

00:13:15.140 --> 00:13:17.980
maybe connected to his daughter,

00:13:17.980 --> 00:13:19.950
and just wanting to,

00:13:19.950 --> 00:13:22.403
I feel like he, would've mentored,

00:13:23.474 --> 00:13:25.168
as he was mentoring you.

00:13:25.168 --> 00:13:30.168
In this program, we have
these children from Kenya,

00:13:30.250 --> 00:13:34.330
and now you have a chance
to give some advice

00:13:34.330 --> 00:13:35.820
and answer some questions,

00:13:35.820 --> 00:13:40.200
and actually, I know there's
a lot of dreamers that have,

00:13:40.200 --> 00:13:41.340
'cause I look at these questions,

00:13:41.340 --> 00:13:44.120
and these are kids that
know what they want

00:13:44.120 --> 00:13:46.470
and they just need to
know how to get there.

00:13:46.470 --> 00:13:48.780
And so I have so many questions.

00:13:48.780 --> 00:13:50.020
I don't wanna be unfair.

00:13:50.020 --> 00:13:54.550
So I wanna make sure that
we get to the questions

00:13:54.550 --> 00:13:55.953
from the kids in Kenya.

00:13:57.320 --> 00:13:58.153
And,

00:13:59.200 --> 00:14:00.887
so, the first question is,

00:14:00.887 --> 00:14:03.137
"At what phase of the filmmaking process

00:14:03.137 --> 00:14:05.770
"do you like to come to a project?"

00:14:05.770 --> 00:14:06.620
- That's a great question.

00:14:06.620 --> 00:14:08.750
I feel like, for me,

00:14:08.750 --> 00:14:11.770
it's as early as possible

00:14:12.680 --> 00:14:15.730
so that I can get a clear
understanding of the story.

00:14:15.730 --> 00:14:17.330
It's also really...

00:14:17.330 --> 00:14:19.440
The story and the characters.

00:14:19.440 --> 00:14:20.440
And it's really interesting

00:14:20.440 --> 00:14:23.130
to see how that continues
to develop over time,

00:14:23.130 --> 00:14:26.400
because there are times
where they start to hone in

00:14:26.400 --> 00:14:27.600
on specific themes,

00:14:27.600 --> 00:14:30.820
or as they're working
on the edit of a film,

00:14:30.820 --> 00:14:32.110
I start to realize, oh,

00:14:32.110 --> 00:14:33.860
some of these other
pieces are falling away,

00:14:33.860 --> 00:14:35.380
because they're not as important.

00:14:35.380 --> 00:14:37.810
Or, they're trying to figure out

00:14:37.810 --> 00:14:40.030
what the heart of the movie is,

00:14:40.030 --> 00:14:41.170
as they're working on it,

00:14:41.170 --> 00:14:44.180
and so if I'm along the
ride in that process,

00:14:44.180 --> 00:14:46.100
I can kind of see what the music

00:14:46.100 --> 00:14:48.450
should be really pointing
to with that as well.

00:14:49.580 --> 00:14:51.070
So I feel like the earlier the better,

00:14:51.070 --> 00:14:53.130
just because we can have
a lot of conversations

00:14:53.130 --> 00:14:57.640
about what they're wanting
the music to really underline

00:14:57.640 --> 00:15:00.120
and emphasize in the storytelling

00:15:00.120 --> 00:15:02.580
and in the experience
with these characters.

00:15:02.580 --> 00:15:04.880
But also I feel like it allows

00:15:04.880 --> 00:15:07.030
for that trial and error period,

00:15:07.030 --> 00:15:09.200
because if I come in too late,

00:15:09.200 --> 00:15:12.300
really we're just trying to get it right,

00:15:12.300 --> 00:15:15.390
and if you don't have that
much time to get it right,

00:15:15.390 --> 00:15:18.180
it's like you're oftentimes

00:15:18.180 --> 00:15:20.920
doing something that's somewhat expected

00:15:20.920 --> 00:15:22.394
or doing something that comes easy.

00:15:22.394 --> 00:15:24.580
You're trying to just get it done quickly,

00:15:24.580 --> 00:15:27.180
but it's hard to do things
that are out of the box

00:15:27.180 --> 00:15:28.740
if you don't have that much time.

00:15:28.740 --> 00:15:31.780
And I love when I have
a lot of time early on

00:15:31.780 --> 00:15:33.430
to try things and fail,

00:15:33.430 --> 00:15:37.000
and directors that really
encourage me to really experiment,

00:15:37.000 --> 00:15:39.200
and if we don't get it right

00:15:39.200 --> 00:15:41.085
with that experimentation, that's okay,

00:15:41.085 --> 00:15:41.918
'cause that experimentation

00:15:41.918 --> 00:15:43.640
might lead us down
somewhere else and all that.

00:15:43.640 --> 00:15:46.970
So having time to have that
sandbox period, for me,

00:15:46.970 --> 00:15:48.390
is really important.

00:15:48.390 --> 00:15:49.750
So earlier the better.

00:15:49.750 --> 00:15:51.233
- Yeah, I agree.

00:15:52.120 --> 00:15:53.462
And this question,

00:15:53.462 --> 00:15:56.470
I think this person probably
already has a job in film,

00:15:56.470 --> 00:15:58.777
because they wanna-
(both laughing)

00:15:58.777 --> 00:16:01.310
"Directors might get to
attached to temp music."

00:16:01.310 --> 00:16:02.885
I call it tempitis.

00:16:02.885 --> 00:16:03.984
- Yep.
(both laughing)

00:16:03.984 --> 00:16:05.680
Tempitis, yeah.
(both laughing)

00:16:05.680 --> 00:16:07.397
And they, so,

00:16:07.397 --> 00:16:09.127
"The temp music that
they use during the edit,

00:16:09.127 --> 00:16:11.860
"how do you introduce
something new to them?"

00:16:11.860 --> 00:16:14.471
- Yeah, I mean, this person already knows.

00:16:14.471 --> 00:16:16.955
(both laughing)
What it is.

00:16:16.955 --> 00:16:18.760
For those of you that don't know,

00:16:18.760 --> 00:16:20.730
temp music is the temporary music

00:16:20.730 --> 00:16:22.260
that's being put in as they're editing

00:16:22.260 --> 00:16:24.910
and trying to figure out what
the sound of the movie is,

00:16:24.910 --> 00:16:28.510
and they take music from other
films and put it in there.

00:16:28.510 --> 00:16:30.120
And for me, I feel like,

00:16:30.120 --> 00:16:33.170
a lot of that's a
conversation with the director

00:16:33.170 --> 00:16:34.940
to earn that trust,

00:16:34.940 --> 00:16:38.970
because really the temp
has created this safety

00:16:38.970 --> 00:16:40.580
where they know that this music

00:16:40.580 --> 00:16:42.800
is hitting the emotion
that they wanna hit,

00:16:42.800 --> 00:16:46.310
and if I can try and have
conversations with them

00:16:46.310 --> 00:16:49.630
that can unlock what it is
that they like about it,

00:16:49.630 --> 00:16:51.410
now there's some directors
that it's difficult,

00:16:51.410 --> 00:16:53.690
because they're, maybe,

00:16:53.690 --> 00:16:55.430
struggling with how to
have that conversation.

00:16:55.430 --> 00:16:57.147
So for them it's like, "I like the temp,

00:16:57.147 --> 00:16:59.300
"whatever you gotta do to
make it feel like that."

00:16:59.300 --> 00:17:02.010
And that becomes a more difficult job.

00:17:02.010 --> 00:17:03.998
And for me, when I'm put in that position,

00:17:03.998 --> 00:17:05.880
I maybe do that process myself,

00:17:05.880 --> 00:17:08.070
where I'm looking at what
maybe is most effective

00:17:08.070 --> 00:17:08.903
about this temp?

00:17:08.903 --> 00:17:10.120
Is it the instrumentation?

00:17:10.120 --> 00:17:10.970
Is it the pace?

00:17:10.970 --> 00:17:13.180
Is it certain moments that it's hitting?

00:17:13.180 --> 00:17:16.560
Is it the key and the frequency?

00:17:16.560 --> 00:17:18.640
Or is it any of those kinds of things

00:17:18.640 --> 00:17:21.300
that might be worth
borrowing from the temp

00:17:21.300 --> 00:17:22.730
to then build my own thing?

00:17:22.730 --> 00:17:26.200
But any director that's
able to have a conversation

00:17:26.200 --> 00:17:29.393
about why is this temp
making you feel that way?

00:17:30.330 --> 00:17:32.820
I've worked a lot with Ava,

00:17:32.820 --> 00:17:34.760
and when we talk about
temp it's really amazing,

00:17:34.760 --> 00:17:37.490
because she talks about
the emotion behind it,

00:17:37.490 --> 00:17:38.680
where she'll say,

00:17:38.680 --> 00:17:42.207
"This temp is making me feel
this way in these moments,"

00:17:42.207 --> 00:17:45.530
and so that way I know that
as long as I hit these moments

00:17:45.530 --> 00:17:48.320
in a similar way emotionally,

00:17:48.320 --> 00:17:49.860
it doesn't even have to be
the same instrumentation

00:17:49.860 --> 00:17:53.070
or any of that or
borrowing too much from it,

00:17:53.070 --> 00:17:56.150
but if we can understand
why it's hitting those beats

00:17:56.150 --> 00:17:57.960
and how it's making her feel,

00:17:57.960 --> 00:18:01.280
then I can kind of deduce
what might be doing that

00:18:01.280 --> 00:18:03.560
and try my own version of that.

00:18:03.560 --> 00:18:05.250
- That's very helpful.

00:18:05.250 --> 00:18:06.447
'Cause sometimes it's just like,

00:18:06.447 --> 00:18:08.557
"Oh yeah, well I just
want, here's Beyonce,

00:18:08.557 --> 00:18:10.757
"and this is just what,
for me as a songwriter,

00:18:10.757 --> 00:18:12.370
"and I don't know why."

00:18:12.370 --> 00:18:13.860
And so having those,

00:18:13.860 --> 00:18:16.537
asking those questions makes
it easier for me to say,

00:18:16.537 --> 00:18:19.487
"Well, what is it that you got from this?

00:18:19.487 --> 00:18:20.767
"How does it make you feel?

00:18:20.767 --> 00:18:22.313
"Is there a lyric?

00:18:23.642 --> 00:18:24.475
"Is the key?

00:18:24.475 --> 00:18:26.380
"Is it the emotions, the
way she's singing it?"

00:18:26.380 --> 00:18:28.950
So that's, yeah, I totally agree.

00:18:28.950 --> 00:18:31.070
- Never thought about the fact
that there's temp with songs.

00:18:31.070 --> 00:18:32.330
I didn't even think about that.

00:18:32.330 --> 00:18:33.163
- Oh God.

00:18:33.163 --> 00:18:35.210
And sometimes, especially because

00:18:36.090 --> 00:18:37.820
the kinds of songs that I write,

00:18:37.820 --> 00:18:40.200
there's usually like one song
that they use all the time,

00:18:40.200 --> 00:18:41.715
and I'll tell you off camera because...

00:18:41.715 --> 00:18:43.400
(both laughing)

00:18:43.400 --> 00:18:45.444
But I'm like, oh, okay, here we go.

00:18:45.444 --> 00:18:46.461
(both laughing)

00:18:46.461 --> 00:18:49.890
And I'm like, I know this
emotion, so I don't know.

00:18:49.890 --> 00:18:51.781
I guess it's a good thing.

00:18:51.781 --> 00:18:53.335
- Yeah, yeah, they know you can hit it-

00:18:53.335 --> 00:18:56.371
(voice muffling)
- Yeah, yeah, absolutely.

00:18:56.371 --> 00:18:59.200
So, what's the most creative technique

00:18:59.200 --> 00:19:02.513
you've been able to bring to
your film career and process?

00:19:03.560 --> 00:19:05.840
- That's a really good question.

00:19:05.840 --> 00:19:06.673
I feel like for me,

00:19:06.673 --> 00:19:11.110
it's my background as an
improviser and as an accompanist,

00:19:11.110 --> 00:19:13.890
just because the thing
I've always loved about

00:19:13.890 --> 00:19:17.410
being a jazz pianist is that
you operate in these two spaces

00:19:17.410 --> 00:19:20.550
where sometimes you're the lead performer,

00:19:20.550 --> 00:19:22.730
especially if you have a trio
or you're playing solo piano,

00:19:22.730 --> 00:19:25.190
but most often you're an accompanist,

00:19:25.190 --> 00:19:26.100
where you're tryin' to figure out

00:19:26.100 --> 00:19:30.900
how to make these harmonies
support the singer

00:19:30.900 --> 00:19:33.740
or support the horn player, whatever.

00:19:33.740 --> 00:19:35.923
And especially when those
people are improvising,

00:19:35.923 --> 00:19:39.010
you're tryin' to figure out
how to fill in the holes

00:19:39.010 --> 00:19:42.690
or how to do something that's
gonna make what they're doing

00:19:42.690 --> 00:19:45.710
make sense or contextualize
what's happening.

00:19:45.710 --> 00:19:46.870
And so for me,

00:19:46.870 --> 00:19:48.580
I approach film scoring the same way

00:19:48.580 --> 00:19:51.220
where I'll watch a scene
over and over again

00:19:51.220 --> 00:19:52.490
without anything,

00:19:52.490 --> 00:19:56.240
and often try to figure
out what the emotion is

00:19:56.240 --> 00:19:57.320
that we're trying to get at

00:19:57.320 --> 00:19:58.810
based on the conversation
with the director

00:19:58.810 --> 00:20:00.150
and based on what I'm seeing.

00:20:00.150 --> 00:20:02.490
And then I go to the piano and improvise

00:20:02.490 --> 00:20:04.600
and to try to find
something that feels like it

00:20:04.600 --> 00:20:07.970
is getting me to a similar emotion.

00:20:07.970 --> 00:20:10.730
And then I just kind of
play along to the screen

00:20:10.730 --> 00:20:12.000
as if I'm accompanying something,

00:20:12.000 --> 00:20:15.410
but it's nice to be able to move around

00:20:15.410 --> 00:20:17.180
and not have to think about,

00:20:17.180 --> 00:20:18.260
and not have to write something out.

00:20:18.260 --> 00:20:21.210
Like I can sit behind a
scene over and over again

00:20:21.210 --> 00:20:22.810
and just try a bunch of different things

00:20:22.810 --> 00:20:25.420
and try a bunch of
different chord changes.

00:20:25.420 --> 00:20:29.020
And then I'm also thinking
in that accompanist way,

00:20:29.020 --> 00:20:31.100
where there might be
moments where I'm like,

00:20:31.100 --> 00:20:32.730
oh, I wanna leave a hole for that line,

00:20:32.730 --> 00:20:34.220
or I wanna emphasize that line,

00:20:34.220 --> 00:20:36.180
or I wanna make a shift under that line.

00:20:36.180 --> 00:20:39.140
And I'm really treating
it like the dialogue

00:20:39.140 --> 00:20:41.570
and the sound is another
set of instruments

00:20:41.570 --> 00:20:43.680
that I'm playing behind.

00:20:43.680 --> 00:20:46.097
And that improvisation
also is incredibly helpful

00:20:46.097 --> 00:20:48.390
in this crazy industry, as you know,

00:20:48.390 --> 00:20:51.070
of people changing their
minds at the last minute

00:20:51.070 --> 00:20:53.360
where there were times
at a recording session

00:20:53.360 --> 00:20:54.193
where a director is like,

00:20:54.193 --> 00:20:56.820
you know what, I actually
never really liked that moment.

00:20:56.820 --> 00:20:58.440
Can we try something else?

00:20:58.440 --> 00:20:59.273
- Right.

00:20:59.273 --> 00:21:00.617
- Oh yeah, that's easy.

00:21:00.617 --> 00:21:01.464
- Yeah, sure, yeah.
(voice muffling)

00:21:01.464 --> 00:21:03.214
- Yeah.
(both laughing)

00:21:03.214 --> 00:21:05.630
- Nothin' like being able to
make those changes quickly

00:21:05.630 --> 00:21:06.870
and not have to worry too much

00:21:06.870 --> 00:21:08.660
and just being able to trust my instincts

00:21:08.660 --> 00:21:12.158
on improvisation have been
really, really valuable, yeah.

00:21:12.158 --> 00:21:14.188
- Yeah, agreed.

00:21:14.188 --> 00:21:15.680
So this...

00:21:15.680 --> 00:21:17.128
I had this question as well.

00:21:17.128 --> 00:21:19.207
Someone asked, "When did you realize

00:21:19.207 --> 00:21:21.907
"that you'd like to apply
your classical training

00:21:21.907 --> 00:21:23.440
"to a film career?"

00:21:23.440 --> 00:21:24.860
'Cause one of my,

00:21:24.860 --> 00:21:26.970
what I noticed is that your,

00:21:26.970 --> 00:21:31.370
over time your scores have
become a bit more classical.

00:21:31.370 --> 00:21:34.990
And so, yeah, so I wanted
to ask that question, too.

00:21:34.990 --> 00:21:35.880
- Yeah, I appreciate that.

00:21:35.880 --> 00:21:39.010
I think that it's something
I've always dreamt about

00:21:39.010 --> 00:21:41.280
because the film music
I fell in love with,

00:21:41.280 --> 00:21:44.030
even at the same time
as I was falling in love

00:21:44.030 --> 00:21:46.250
with all the jazz records
I was falling in love with,

00:21:46.250 --> 00:21:49.030
like Oscar Peterson or Wynton Kelly

00:21:49.030 --> 00:21:50.980
or Miles Davis, Herbie Hancock,

00:21:50.980 --> 00:21:53.700
I was also listening
mostly to John Williams

00:21:53.700 --> 00:21:55.710
or Howard Shore or James Newton Howard,

00:21:55.710 --> 00:21:57.110
or like these composers that

00:21:58.240 --> 00:22:00.730
I definitely had also a
huge love and soft spot

00:22:00.730 --> 00:22:01.660
for Terence Blanchard

00:22:01.660 --> 00:22:05.053
and composers that were using
jazz elements in their scores.

00:22:05.053 --> 00:22:07.460
But when I thought of film scoring,

00:22:07.460 --> 00:22:08.760
I thought of John Williams

00:22:08.760 --> 00:22:12.230
or I thought of these movies
that were shaping my childhood.

00:22:12.230 --> 00:22:17.230
And so I always dreamt of
trying to get more into

00:22:17.290 --> 00:22:18.530
that type of space,

00:22:18.530 --> 00:22:20.860
and as you were just kind of saying,

00:22:20.860 --> 00:22:23.340
it's really interesting to kinda navigate

00:22:23.340 --> 00:22:25.900
when you're known for something,

00:22:25.900 --> 00:22:27.410
when people come to you for that thing,

00:22:27.410 --> 00:22:32.410
and I remember my first
couple of projects, it was,

00:22:32.890 --> 00:22:34.750
I was just very fortunate
to have directors

00:22:34.750 --> 00:22:36.503
that took a chance on
me doing something else,

00:22:36.503 --> 00:22:39.940
because I remember my first few meetings,

00:22:39.940 --> 00:22:42.270
I would go into a studio meeting,

00:22:42.270 --> 00:22:43.660
and those studio heads would be like,

00:22:43.660 --> 00:22:46.720
oh, we don't really have
any jazz stuff or any-

00:22:46.720 --> 00:22:47.660
- Oh, wow, yeah.

00:22:47.660 --> 00:22:49.620
- Yeah or they'd send me-
(voice muffling)

00:22:49.620 --> 00:22:50.980
Hip hop stuff just because

00:22:50.980 --> 00:22:53.063
that seemed like it probably made sense.

00:22:53.063 --> 00:22:55.820
(both laughing)

00:22:55.820 --> 00:22:57.230
And it was,

00:22:57.230 --> 00:22:59.877
that Kobe Bryant documentary
is because my friend was like,

00:22:59.877 --> 00:23:01.197
"You're a dope musician,

00:23:01.197 --> 00:23:04.397
"and I know that if I give
you context and temp music

00:23:04.397 --> 00:23:06.677
"and we talk about Cliff Martinez

00:23:06.677 --> 00:23:09.267
"or Philip Glass or Steve
Reich or that kind of music,

00:23:09.267 --> 00:23:10.547
"that you're gonna be able to get that,

00:23:10.547 --> 00:23:11.590
"or (voice muffling)."

00:23:11.590 --> 00:23:14.880
Justin Simien, even though a
lot of that has jazz in it,

00:23:14.880 --> 00:23:15.817
he was also like,

00:23:15.817 --> 00:23:18.227
"I want jazz, but I also want Ravel,

00:23:18.227 --> 00:23:20.547
"or I want things that feel like Chopin

00:23:20.547 --> 00:23:22.520
"or like that kind of material."

00:23:22.520 --> 00:23:25.370
So I feel like a lot of
that was really helpful

00:23:25.370 --> 00:23:27.330
in giving me the experience
of writing that way.

00:23:27.330 --> 00:23:28.760
And same with Kobe, actually.

00:23:28.760 --> 00:23:31.853
Kobe, for his audiobooks,

00:23:32.940 --> 00:23:35.390
John Williams was always
his template in his mind

00:23:35.390 --> 00:23:37.350
of what score would sound like.

00:23:37.350 --> 00:23:39.960
And so, the stuff I had
to write for that was

00:23:39.960 --> 00:23:41.900
trying my best to write that Harry Potter

00:23:41.900 --> 00:23:43.070
type of style of writing,

00:23:43.070 --> 00:23:46.250
which for me just meant
studying those scores

00:23:46.250 --> 00:23:48.480
and studying how to write in that style.

00:23:48.480 --> 00:23:50.907
So I feel like by the time something like

00:23:50.907 --> 00:23:53.520
"Bridgerton" came along, which is like

00:23:53.520 --> 00:23:55.583
completely this classical sound.

00:23:56.673 --> 00:23:58.410
A lot of it was like a combination

00:23:58.410 --> 00:24:00.290
of me always wanting to do that,

00:24:00.290 --> 00:24:02.900
also having filmmakers that gave me

00:24:02.900 --> 00:24:04.390
the space and trust to do that,

00:24:04.390 --> 00:24:06.390
just because, like "Bridgerton" came along

00:24:06.390 --> 00:24:09.171
because I had done a Shondaland
show a few years ago,

00:24:09.171 --> 00:24:10.970
that sounds nothing like that,

00:24:10.970 --> 00:24:14.900
and they just, again, trusted
that as a good musician,

00:24:14.900 --> 00:24:17.430
I might be able to figure that sound out.

00:24:17.430 --> 00:24:20.160
And for me, it was all
about studying that.

00:24:20.160 --> 00:24:21.633
By the time I got "Bridgerton"

00:24:21.633 --> 00:24:23.490
I had done so much studying that I felt

00:24:23.490 --> 00:24:25.910
much more confident and
comfortable in that kind of space.

00:24:25.910 --> 00:24:27.580
So, I feel like it's like,

00:24:27.580 --> 00:24:30.030
I know people, I've always
heard my whole life like that,

00:24:30.030 --> 00:24:30.863
you've probably heard it,

00:24:30.863 --> 00:24:33.000
that success is when
preparation meets opportunity.

00:24:33.000 --> 00:24:34.130
- Preparation, yeah.

00:24:34.130 --> 00:24:34.963
- Yeah, and so for me-
- Yeah, absolutely.

00:24:34.963 --> 00:24:36.040
I'm just like, always like,

00:24:36.040 --> 00:24:37.343
how can I continue to prepare?

00:24:37.343 --> 00:24:40.390
Because who knows when that
opportunity's gonna come along?

00:24:40.390 --> 00:24:42.740
- Yeah. I mean, if you (laughing)

00:24:42.740 --> 00:24:47.390
I am positive that
Shonda Rhimes could see,

00:24:47.390 --> 00:24:48.920
I feel like she's this person that,

00:24:48.920 --> 00:24:50.620
'cause she's all knowing and all seeing,

00:24:50.620 --> 00:24:52.200
she could see-
(Kris laughing)

00:24:52.200 --> 00:24:54.500
So she saw that in you and knew that,

00:24:54.500 --> 00:24:55.940
and gave you that opportunity.

00:24:55.940 --> 00:24:58.010
'Cause oftentimes we go into
meetings, like you said,

00:24:58.010 --> 00:25:00.140
and this, "Oh, can you
write this hip hop song?"

00:25:00.140 --> 00:25:00.973
Or can you...

00:25:00.973 --> 00:25:03.510
I love that when people take chances,

00:25:03.510 --> 00:25:05.970
and that's how we blossom,
that's how we grow.

00:25:05.970 --> 00:25:10.500
That's how we get to just stretch.

00:25:10.500 --> 00:25:11.580
I love that.

00:25:11.580 --> 00:25:12.480
I love that.

00:25:13.598 --> 00:25:15.960
So, what questions do you ask yourself

00:25:15.960 --> 00:25:17.770
that help you to decide

00:25:17.770 --> 00:25:20.113
the style of score you'll be creating?

00:25:21.940 --> 00:25:26.020
- Well, I guess if it's
on a specific project,

00:25:26.020 --> 00:25:29.520
most of that is dictated by
conversations with the director.

00:25:29.520 --> 00:25:32.680
Like, oftentimes it's
talking to him or her

00:25:32.680 --> 00:25:37.460
about what they might envision

00:25:37.460 --> 00:25:40.500
in terms of what this character listens to

00:25:40.500 --> 00:25:45.500
or what kind of music is
happening in this environment,

00:25:46.950 --> 00:25:48.780
or if there's any music,

00:25:48.780 --> 00:25:50.580
oftentimes directors I work with

00:25:50.580 --> 00:25:52.360
were listening to music
when they were writing,

00:25:52.360 --> 00:25:53.840
if they were also the
writer of the script.

00:25:53.840 --> 00:25:54.757
So I often ask,

00:25:54.757 --> 00:25:56.370
"What other stuff have
you been listening to?"

00:25:56.370 --> 00:25:57.470
That's really helpful.

00:25:58.970 --> 00:26:00.750
I also really love
creating these boundaries

00:26:00.750 --> 00:26:02.780
that I can then play in where,

00:26:02.780 --> 00:26:04.390
for a movie like "King Richard,"

00:26:04.390 --> 00:26:06.410
I thought about the fact that

00:26:06.410 --> 00:26:08.120
it might be interesting to pull elements

00:26:08.120 --> 00:26:11.370
from this world sonically,

00:26:11.370 --> 00:26:13.050
thinking about the sound of tennis

00:26:13.050 --> 00:26:15.470
and the sound of the
ball hitting the strings

00:26:15.470 --> 00:26:16.303
and things like that.

00:26:16.303 --> 00:26:18.500
- [Taura] Mmm, yeah.
(voice muffling)

00:26:18.500 --> 00:26:20.920
- Prepared piano or all these other things

00:26:20.920 --> 00:26:23.900
that kind of maybe would
create a nice texture

00:26:23.900 --> 00:26:25.970
that made sense for me with the film.

00:26:25.970 --> 00:26:28.200
- Yes, so when you,

00:26:28.200 --> 00:26:30.388
when we are talking
about the kinds of films,

00:26:30.388 --> 00:26:32.950
the sound that you're developing is,

00:26:32.950 --> 00:26:34.200
is moving into classical.

00:26:34.200 --> 00:26:35.230
It is classical.

00:26:35.230 --> 00:26:38.680
So when you, will you score other films

00:26:38.680 --> 00:26:40.740
that will take you completely out of that?

00:26:40.740 --> 00:26:42.430
You know how John Williams has a sound.

00:26:42.430 --> 00:26:44.543
Are you creating that Kris Bower sound?

00:26:45.471 --> 00:26:46.304
- I don't know.

00:26:46.304 --> 00:26:48.463
I think, I often look at,

00:26:49.450 --> 00:26:52.370
John Williams, I've
studied his career a lot,

00:26:52.370 --> 00:26:54.270
and I think the thing that
was really fascinating for him

00:26:54.270 --> 00:26:57.370
is when he was around my age and in his,

00:26:57.370 --> 00:26:59.720
I'd say, like 20s to 30s,

00:26:59.720 --> 00:27:02.400
he was doing a lot of studio work

00:27:02.400 --> 00:27:04.580
where he was just kind of writing

00:27:04.580 --> 00:27:06.250
the style of these different people.

00:27:06.250 --> 00:27:09.210
And I think also at the time there was,

00:27:09.210 --> 00:27:12.510
you saw this old Hollywood
style of orchestral writing

00:27:12.510 --> 00:27:13.343
and things like that.

00:27:13.343 --> 00:27:15.560
But within that, he was writing

00:27:15.560 --> 00:27:17.050
all these different versions of it

00:27:17.050 --> 00:27:20.300
and really trying to write
different types of music.

00:27:20.300 --> 00:27:23.550
So I feel like I'm kind of
in that period of my life

00:27:23.550 --> 00:27:27.480
where I'm just open to,
whatever's going to challenge me.

00:27:27.480 --> 00:27:30.220
I think, again, something
else that Kobe mentioned

00:27:30.220 --> 00:27:32.047
to me a lot was like,

00:27:32.047 --> 00:27:33.940
before you say yes to something,

00:27:33.940 --> 00:27:36.310
think about how that's
going to challenge you

00:27:36.310 --> 00:27:39.550
and make you a better
composer by the end of it.

00:27:39.550 --> 00:27:42.860
And if you feel like that's
gonna do that, then take it.

00:27:42.860 --> 00:27:45.500
And so oftentimes, even projects that are,

00:27:45.500 --> 00:27:47.470
often projects that my team is like,

00:27:47.470 --> 00:27:49.178
why are you doing that?

00:27:49.178 --> 00:27:50.011
(voice muffling)

00:27:50.011 --> 00:27:52.260
I wanna do it because
it's gonna gimme a chance

00:27:52.260 --> 00:27:55.240
to exercise this muscle
that I've never had.

00:27:55.240 --> 00:27:56.820
Like before scoring "Space Jam,"

00:27:56.820 --> 00:27:58.500
I wanted to score raising "Raising Dion"

00:27:58.500 --> 00:27:59.910
'cause I'd never done something

00:27:59.910 --> 00:28:04.910
that demanded a bigger
sound or an action sound.

00:28:05.000 --> 00:28:08.190
And so for me it was just,
what's gonna make me better?

00:28:08.190 --> 00:28:11.300
And how can I take those
things on to continue to grow?

00:28:11.300 --> 00:28:12.580
So maybe at some point

00:28:12.580 --> 00:28:14.780
I'll have a sound that's identifiable,

00:28:14.780 --> 00:28:16.190
but I feel like right now it's just,

00:28:16.190 --> 00:28:17.130
I'm still in student mode.
- Developing.

00:28:17.130 --> 00:28:19.140
- Yeah, yeah.
- Yeah, yeah.

00:28:19.140 --> 00:28:21.001
- That's amazing that you feel that way.

00:28:21.001 --> 00:28:23.110
(both laughing)

00:28:23.110 --> 00:28:25.540
'Cause you are...

00:28:25.540 --> 00:28:26.590
I hear...

00:28:26.590 --> 00:28:29.550
I don't know how I missed
that you did "Raising Dion."

00:28:29.550 --> 00:28:31.922
I just, I binge watched it yesterday.

00:28:31.922 --> 00:28:33.119
- Oh thank you.

00:28:33.119 --> 00:28:36.160
- I mean, two days, two seasons.

00:28:36.160 --> 00:28:38.290
- Wow, oh wow, that's amazing.

00:28:38.290 --> 00:28:41.370
- I had to check out and just recenter,

00:28:41.370 --> 00:28:43.660
and I did it with "Raising Dion."

00:28:43.660 --> 00:28:45.354
So sidebar.

00:28:45.354 --> 00:28:46.216
(both laughing)

00:28:46.216 --> 00:28:47.270
- Appreciate it.
- That's amazing.

00:28:47.270 --> 00:28:48.103
I love that kid.

00:28:48.103 --> 00:28:49.640
It's just like such a cool...

00:28:49.640 --> 00:28:50.763
I really love it.

00:28:51.930 --> 00:28:54.567
So my next question is

00:28:54.567 --> 00:28:57.627
"What's the one film score
that gets stuck in your head?"

00:28:58.910 --> 00:29:00.720
- I mean there's definitely a lot.

00:29:00.720 --> 00:29:01.553
I mean,

00:29:02.640 --> 00:29:05.460
they're cliche just 'cause
they are what they are.

00:29:05.460 --> 00:29:07.350
I feel like "Jurassic
Park" and "Star Wars"

00:29:07.350 --> 00:29:10.110
are probably two that
really stick in my mind,

00:29:10.110 --> 00:29:11.160
just because they're,

00:29:12.430 --> 00:29:13.263
yeah, just-
- Yeah,

00:29:13.263 --> 00:29:15.450
- Listened to them so
much when I was a kid,

00:29:15.450 --> 00:29:18.320
and they were such big
part of my childhood,

00:29:18.320 --> 00:29:19.500
and I feel like for all of us,

00:29:19.500 --> 00:29:21.500
it's such a nostalgic feeling
that you get immediately

00:29:21.500 --> 00:29:24.840
when you hear any of
those chords or harmonies.

00:29:24.840 --> 00:29:25.777
And also "E.T."

00:29:25.777 --> 00:29:28.000
- "E.T." Yes.

00:29:28.000 --> 00:29:30.550
- The piano theme, the
flying theme from "E.T."

00:29:30.550 --> 00:29:33.030
is like something that sticks with me.

00:29:33.030 --> 00:29:35.130
So yeah, John Williams is always my go to.

00:29:35.967 --> 00:29:37.510
- Yes, mine, too.

00:29:37.510 --> 00:29:39.060
I loved...

00:29:39.060 --> 00:29:40.080
When I was a kid,

00:29:40.080 --> 00:29:42.290
I wanted to date two
people when I grew up,

00:29:42.290 --> 00:29:44.483
and I was sure that I was gonna date E.T.

00:29:45.348 --> 00:29:46.710
(Kris laughing)
(voice muffling)

00:29:46.710 --> 00:29:48.110
My mom was like, "Okay girl."

00:29:48.110 --> 00:29:50.094
(voice muffling)
(Kris laughing)

00:29:50.094 --> 00:29:50.927
E.T. and Scooby Doo,

00:29:50.927 --> 00:29:52.541
I'm gonna be with them when I grow up.

00:29:52.541 --> 00:29:53.936
(voice muffling)
(Kris laughing)

00:29:53.936 --> 00:29:54.769
(both laughing)

00:29:54.769 --> 00:29:57.119
So obviously, I watched
a lot of movies and TV.

00:29:58.460 --> 00:29:59.971
So my next question is

00:29:59.971 --> 00:30:02.577
"The director has a new
idea that contradicts

00:30:02.577 --> 00:30:05.340
"an earlier conversation
that you had with them."

00:30:05.340 --> 00:30:07.537
How do you handle those
tricky conversations

00:30:07.537 --> 00:30:10.967
"without compromising your
own vision for the score?"

00:30:12.160 --> 00:30:13.810
- I think for me,

00:30:13.810 --> 00:30:16.914
the vision is whatever serves the story.

00:30:16.914 --> 00:30:19.313
So there's so many times where I have,

00:30:20.470 --> 00:30:21.520
you take the time to come up

00:30:21.520 --> 00:30:23.260
with a really clear vision for something,

00:30:23.260 --> 00:30:25.057
and then I take it to the
director and they're like,

00:30:25.057 --> 00:30:26.080
"No, that's not it."

00:30:26.080 --> 00:30:28.870
And then for me I feel like

00:30:28.870 --> 00:30:31.240
we are all trusting this director

00:30:31.240 --> 00:30:33.670
with the vision of what this story is,

00:30:33.670 --> 00:30:36.380
and the best directors are
also trusting the story

00:30:36.380 --> 00:30:39.790
to be what it wants to be and to kind of,

00:30:39.790 --> 00:30:41.570
following that as it's north star.

00:30:41.570 --> 00:30:43.960
And so I feel like for me,

00:30:43.960 --> 00:30:46.270
whatever the director says is what it is.

00:30:46.270 --> 00:30:49.280
And so oftentimes a director
changing their mind,

00:30:49.280 --> 00:30:51.880
for me, has always led me to

00:30:53.356 --> 00:30:55.630
a much stronger
representation in the music,

00:30:55.630 --> 00:30:57.320
in the score where like,

00:30:57.320 --> 00:30:59.130
there are times where we're maybe

00:30:59.130 --> 00:31:00.767
scoring a certain
character in a certain way

00:31:00.767 --> 00:31:03.210
and we realize that we need
to be a bit more complex

00:31:03.210 --> 00:31:07.540
or not as one dimensional
with how we're doing that.

00:31:07.540 --> 00:31:10.920
Or by the time we get too
far along into the film,

00:31:10.920 --> 00:31:12.570
we realize that the way we've been scoring

00:31:12.570 --> 00:31:14.176
this relationship or this character

00:31:14.176 --> 00:31:16.130
doesn't pay off in the way we want it to.

00:31:16.130 --> 00:31:18.340
So now we have to go back
and try to figure that out.

00:31:18.340 --> 00:31:22.030
And also at the end of
the day, we're all humans.

00:31:22.030 --> 00:31:25.510
There's so many times
where I changed my mind

00:31:25.510 --> 00:31:29.460
about life, about so
many different things,

00:31:29.460 --> 00:31:33.757
that it's always about
us being open to that.

00:31:33.757 --> 00:31:37.339
And I feel like allowing
for that human aspect

00:31:37.339 --> 00:31:40.080
of people feeling differently
once they've had time

00:31:40.080 --> 00:31:41.190
to really sit with something,

00:31:41.190 --> 00:31:43.835
only allows for better art.

00:31:43.835 --> 00:31:46.260
- Yeah, and taking nothing personal.

00:31:46.260 --> 00:31:49.520
I feel like in life and film,

00:31:49.520 --> 00:31:51.350
whenever I go into a project,

00:31:51.350 --> 00:31:53.920
I know that if someone
says, I don't want that

00:31:53.920 --> 00:31:55.693
or I want this temp song,

00:31:56.700 --> 00:31:59.330
just like that, I just
don't take it personally.

00:31:59.330 --> 00:32:03.910
And also being willing to do
as many revisions as possible,

00:32:03.910 --> 00:32:06.133
just 'cause I know it really gets better.

00:32:07.100 --> 00:32:09.070
Every now and again, you may,

00:32:09.070 --> 00:32:11.030
oh, I miss the original version,

00:32:11.030 --> 00:32:14.530
but I usually really,
really like the process

00:32:14.530 --> 00:32:18.764
of going to doing it over
until it gets, we get it right.

00:32:18.764 --> 00:32:19.941
- Yeah, 100%.
- Yeah.

00:32:19.941 --> 00:32:21.510
- Yeah, that's great, yeah.

00:32:21.510 --> 00:32:26.510
- So "How do you find the
film's musical language?

00:32:26.777 --> 00:32:29.207
"And what about the
films repeating motifs?"

00:32:31.770 --> 00:32:36.600
- So I feel like the
motifs usually come from

00:32:36.600 --> 00:32:38.450
once I've had those
conversations with the director

00:32:38.450 --> 00:32:41.323
about what's most important to them,

00:32:42.800 --> 00:32:44.990
we usually try to focus on those moments.

00:32:44.990 --> 00:32:49.440
And for me, I'll always
watch the film through

00:32:49.440 --> 00:32:51.880
until I find a moment that speaks to me

00:32:51.880 --> 00:32:53.910
on a really visceral level,

00:32:53.910 --> 00:32:57.420
where with "Green Book,"

00:32:57.420 --> 00:33:00.470
it was this moment where
Mahershala's character,

00:33:00.470 --> 00:33:02.250
Don Shirley, was standing outside

00:33:02.250 --> 00:33:03.970
watching these black field workers

00:33:03.970 --> 00:33:05.670
while he's being driven by this white man,

00:33:05.670 --> 00:33:06.720
and you see this

00:33:09.400 --> 00:33:10.720
solitude in his eyes,

00:33:10.720 --> 00:33:12.890
and this almost tinge of sadness or pain.

00:33:12.890 --> 00:33:15.570
And that was the moment that felt like,

00:33:15.570 --> 00:33:17.440
I feel like I can
immediately write something

00:33:17.440 --> 00:33:19.810
that maybe can get at that feeling.

00:33:19.810 --> 00:33:22.960
And then that theme was now his theme,

00:33:22.960 --> 00:33:24.290
and now I can look at any moment

00:33:24.290 --> 00:33:26.530
where we wanna work with his theme

00:33:26.530 --> 00:33:29.213
and try to put that in those other places.

00:33:30.140 --> 00:33:30.973
Same with "King Richard."

00:33:30.973 --> 00:33:32.418
It was like seeing this father

00:33:32.418 --> 00:33:34.330
being told no over and over again,

00:33:34.330 --> 00:33:37.040
and then just keeping a
brave face for his kids

00:33:37.040 --> 00:33:38.360
and continuing to push his kids

00:33:38.360 --> 00:33:40.690
in this positive, beautiful way.

00:33:40.690 --> 00:33:43.457
And with that, that was a
moment that really spoke to me.

00:33:43.457 --> 00:33:46.330
And so once I had that theme,
that was Richard's theme.

00:33:46.330 --> 00:33:47.620
So then I go through and figure out

00:33:47.620 --> 00:33:49.470
where else can we use Richard's theme?

00:33:49.470 --> 00:33:52.870
And then sometimes when I
look at the other themes,

00:33:52.870 --> 00:33:54.870
it's how does that theme then play with

00:33:54.870 --> 00:33:56.760
the theme that has
already been established

00:33:56.760 --> 00:34:00.140
or what are we trying to
say with that character?

00:34:00.140 --> 00:34:02.630
And kinda do the same thing.

00:34:02.630 --> 00:34:04.500
Thinking about something like "Space Jam."

00:34:04.500 --> 00:34:07.030
It was this relationship
between LeBron and his son,

00:34:07.030 --> 00:34:10.560
but also Al G., the Don Cheadle character,

00:34:10.560 --> 00:34:14.880
was like all about ego
and toxic masculinity,

00:34:14.880 --> 00:34:16.050
essentially, all these things.

00:34:16.050 --> 00:34:18.480
And so, I feel like both of those worlds

00:34:18.480 --> 00:34:21.670
are worlds that I could
easily kind of put my head,

00:34:21.670 --> 00:34:22.610
get into that head space

00:34:22.610 --> 00:34:24.220
and write something from that place.

00:34:24.220 --> 00:34:26.290
And then once I have those
themes that are really strong,

00:34:26.290 --> 00:34:27.940
it's just continuing to follow the story

00:34:27.940 --> 00:34:29.570
with those characters or those motifs,

00:34:29.570 --> 00:34:30.403
'cause there are even times

00:34:30.403 --> 00:34:32.550
where that character might not be present,

00:34:32.550 --> 00:34:34.450
but whatever we're talking
about in that scene

00:34:34.450 --> 00:34:35.820
might have to do with that character.

00:34:35.820 --> 00:34:39.780
And so, using that theme there,
and by the time I do that,

00:34:39.780 --> 00:34:41.530
it often feels like a lot of the movie

00:34:41.530 --> 00:34:43.490
has kind of been scored at that point.

00:34:43.490 --> 00:34:46.500
And then it's about kind
of filling in the holes.

00:34:46.500 --> 00:34:48.280
- Yeah, that's, okay.

00:34:48.280 --> 00:34:51.250
So you just hit us with like three movies

00:34:51.250 --> 00:34:52.720
that I know I love,

00:34:52.720 --> 00:34:55.013
and I'm sure everyone watching does, too.

00:34:56.010 --> 00:34:57.810
I have a two-part question, I think.

00:34:58.760 --> 00:35:00.540
What musical story do you wanna tell?

00:35:00.540 --> 00:35:02.630
I know with Don Shirley,

00:35:02.630 --> 00:35:04.400
is there someone else that you,

00:35:04.400 --> 00:35:07.860
or can you even share that, like what...

00:35:07.860 --> 00:35:11.500
It could even be like the story
of how a city feels to you.

00:35:11.500 --> 00:35:13.260
It doesn't have to be a
certain, you know what I mean?

00:35:13.260 --> 00:35:15.720
Do you have a musical
story that you haven't told

00:35:15.720 --> 00:35:17.970
that you personally wanna tell?

00:35:17.970 --> 00:35:19.510
- Yeah, I don't...

00:35:19.510 --> 00:35:20.840
There are definitely a lot of musicians

00:35:20.840 --> 00:35:22.070
that I really would love to...

00:35:22.070 --> 00:35:23.460
I'm just curious about their lives

00:35:23.460 --> 00:35:24.570
and how to tell that story,

00:35:24.570 --> 00:35:28.590
like thinking about Duke
Ellington or Billy Strayhorn

00:35:28.590 --> 00:35:31.650
or Thelonious Monk or Louis Armstrong.

00:35:31.650 --> 00:35:33.680
I feel like Louis Armstrong
is really fascinating to me,

00:35:33.680 --> 00:35:35.270
because I mean him and Duke Ellington,

00:35:35.270 --> 00:35:37.830
they were these icons,

00:35:37.830 --> 00:35:39.837
and these like pop icons and celebrities

00:35:39.837 --> 00:35:43.040
at a time where they
were also experiencing

00:35:43.040 --> 00:35:45.169
what it was like to be a
black man in this country

00:35:45.169 --> 00:35:48.340
in a way that I just don't know how

00:35:48.340 --> 00:35:52.400
Louis Armstrong was able
to be as warm and uplifting

00:35:52.400 --> 00:35:54.940
and such a beautiful spirit and energy

00:35:56.410 --> 00:35:57.350
for the rest of the world

00:35:57.350 --> 00:35:58.660
when he was dealing with that personally.

00:35:58.660 --> 00:36:01.830
So I feel like those stories,
maybe as far as musicians,

00:36:01.830 --> 00:36:03.030
immediately come to mind.

00:36:03.030 --> 00:36:04.170
- Yeah, yeah.

00:36:04.170 --> 00:36:05.593
I see that for you.

00:36:06.840 --> 00:36:08.714
The other, it's a fun question,

00:36:08.714 --> 00:36:10.098
but I don't know if you
can really answer it.

00:36:10.098 --> 00:36:11.178
(Kris laughing)

00:36:11.178 --> 00:36:12.011
Do you have a favorite?

00:36:12.011 --> 00:36:14.060
Do you have a favorite
film that you've worked on?

00:36:14.060 --> 00:36:17.470
I mean, you've worked on
TV and film, but film,

00:36:17.470 --> 00:36:19.693
do you have a favorite score?

00:36:20.960 --> 00:36:22.420
- You know, not really.

00:36:22.420 --> 00:36:23.253
It's really, like you said,

00:36:23.253 --> 00:36:24.207
it's really tough to answer that.

00:36:24.207 --> 00:36:25.890
I think that generally

00:36:25.890 --> 00:36:29.430
it's usually whatever is like
the last thing I worked on

00:36:29.430 --> 00:36:31.110
kind of feels like (voice muffling)

00:36:31.110 --> 00:36:34.080
feel most connected to and most proud of,

00:36:34.080 --> 00:36:35.660
especially 'cause it always represents

00:36:35.660 --> 00:36:38.040
some sort of maturation or something.

00:36:38.040 --> 00:36:39.570
So I do think "King Richard," in that way,

00:36:39.570 --> 00:36:42.950
is probably right now one that
really like sticks with me,

00:36:42.950 --> 00:36:44.870
but there's always an element of each one

00:36:44.870 --> 00:36:46.800
that I feel proud of and I'm sure

00:36:46.800 --> 00:36:49.023
the next one's gonna be my favorite.

00:36:49.986 --> 00:36:50.819
- And that, I'll tell you,

00:36:50.819 --> 00:36:53.491
I went to see that movie three times.

00:36:53.491 --> 00:36:54.660
- [Kris] Oh wow, oh man.

00:36:54.660 --> 00:36:55.773
- I, every time,

00:36:56.610 --> 00:36:58.830
no one was watching, and they were,

00:36:58.830 --> 00:37:00.080
because like, oh...

00:37:00.080 --> 00:37:01.930
Because it just pulls on you
in so many different ways,

00:37:01.930 --> 00:37:05.360
just beautifully executed,
just beautifully written,

00:37:05.360 --> 00:37:07.700
beautiful, all the acting,
everything was amazing,

00:37:07.700 --> 00:37:09.440
script, everything.

00:37:09.440 --> 00:37:10.993
So the other question is,

00:37:12.050 --> 00:37:14.220
the last question from this series,

00:37:14.220 --> 00:37:16.447
and we have a couple more,

00:37:16.447 --> 00:37:19.217
"How is composing for
film similar or different

00:37:19.217 --> 00:37:21.157
"from composing for other mediums

00:37:21.157 --> 00:37:23.607
"like classical or jazz performances?"

00:37:24.560 --> 00:37:25.393
- That's interesting.

00:37:25.393 --> 00:37:26.226
I feel like,

00:37:27.120 --> 00:37:30.100
one is the fact that the film

00:37:30.100 --> 00:37:32.070
you are always a part of something else.

00:37:32.070 --> 00:37:36.380
And so oftentimes there are times where

00:37:36.380 --> 00:37:39.400
I might wanna do something
because as a musician

00:37:39.400 --> 00:37:44.150
I really wanna kind of exercise an idea

00:37:44.150 --> 00:37:46.390
or build something out and kind of

00:37:46.390 --> 00:37:47.910
make something feel really big,

00:37:47.910 --> 00:37:49.860
or whatever it might be, or busy,

00:37:49.860 --> 00:37:51.490
and that's not gonna be the best thing

00:37:51.490 --> 00:37:55.040
to serve the action or
the story or the moment.

00:37:55.040 --> 00:37:58.900
And so, even though I
love being an accompanist,

00:37:58.900 --> 00:38:00.430
as a film composer, you're always that.

00:38:00.430 --> 00:38:02.154
And I think in the concert space,

00:38:02.154 --> 00:38:06.410
there's nothing that you're
accompanying in that way

00:38:06.410 --> 00:38:09.810
in terms of being able to write whatever.

00:38:09.810 --> 00:38:12.970
And oftentimes that can be
a bit more difficult for me,

00:38:12.970 --> 00:38:15.920
where thinking about a couple of

00:38:15.920 --> 00:38:19.780
classical pieces I've written
in the last few years,

00:38:19.780 --> 00:38:21.010
written a couple of concertos

00:38:21.010 --> 00:38:23.220
and those are so difficult for me

00:38:23.220 --> 00:38:25.880
because now every second is something,

00:38:25.880 --> 00:38:27.330
can be something else.

00:38:27.330 --> 00:38:29.280
And oftentimes it's really helpful

00:38:29.280 --> 00:38:32.120
to have something that
you're accompanying.

00:38:32.120 --> 00:38:34.990
'Cause then you're just
focusing on supporting something

00:38:34.990 --> 00:38:36.580
instead of trying to

00:38:36.580 --> 00:38:40.050
constantly think of how to fill the space.

00:38:40.050 --> 00:38:42.850
But I think the similarity for me is also

00:38:42.850 --> 00:38:46.690
always based in storytelling
and emotion where

00:38:46.690 --> 00:38:50.380
even as a jazz pianist,
if I was improvising,

00:38:50.380 --> 00:38:52.519
it was my turn to improvise,

00:38:52.519 --> 00:38:55.110
for me I always had to feel like

00:38:55.110 --> 00:38:56.350
what is my mood right now?

00:38:56.350 --> 00:38:57.679
What's the mood of the space?

00:38:57.679 --> 00:39:01.013
Where do I want to
improvise from emotionally?

00:39:01.970 --> 00:39:04.760
And that just became a north star,

00:39:04.760 --> 00:39:06.100
and anytime I didn't have that,

00:39:06.100 --> 00:39:08.801
I just felt like I was just playing,

00:39:08.801 --> 00:39:10.470
not connected to my heart.

00:39:10.470 --> 00:39:12.670
It was like mostly just muscle memory

00:39:12.670 --> 00:39:14.730
or playing from my brain.

00:39:14.730 --> 00:39:16.300
And I feel like even when I'm

00:39:16.300 --> 00:39:18.250
writing something for that space,

00:39:18.250 --> 00:39:21.210
it's the same thing where I
need to have some sort of story

00:39:21.210 --> 00:39:23.640
or idea or emotion that
I'm trying to capture,

00:39:23.640 --> 00:39:25.210
because then I feel
like I have a north star

00:39:25.210 --> 00:39:28.570
that I can go after instead
of just writing something

00:39:28.570 --> 00:39:33.570
to be musically impressive or
like an exercise, essentially.

00:39:34.240 --> 00:39:35.357
- Yeah, got it.

00:39:35.357 --> 00:39:37.767
"So what is your favorite
musical composition?

00:39:37.767 --> 00:39:39.543
"And what makes it so good?"

00:39:40.570 --> 00:39:41.760
- That's a really tough question.

00:39:41.760 --> 00:39:46.277
I think the answer I always
come to is "Lush Life,"

00:39:46.277 --> 00:39:48.833
"Lush Life," by Billy Strayhorn, which is,

00:39:49.817 --> 00:39:52.730
it's a really heart
wrenching, beautiful song.

00:39:52.730 --> 00:39:53.880
My favorite recording of it

00:39:53.880 --> 00:39:55.723
is Johnny Hartman and John Coltrane.

00:39:56.770 --> 00:40:00.190
And it's this sad song about a guy

00:40:00.190 --> 00:40:01.480
who lives a lush life,

00:40:01.480 --> 00:40:05.140
like lives a life drinking,
at dive bars, and all that.

00:40:05.140 --> 00:40:08.660
And I fell in love with the song,

00:40:08.660 --> 00:40:10.997
and then afterward learned
that Billy Strayhorn

00:40:10.997 --> 00:40:13.010
was 17 when he wrote it,

00:40:13.010 --> 00:40:16.360
and learning about the
pain that he experienced

00:40:16.360 --> 00:40:18.930
as a gay man, a gay man at that time,

00:40:18.930 --> 00:40:21.000
a gay black man at that time.

00:40:21.000 --> 00:40:22.410
- Wow.
- Such a heaviness

00:40:22.410 --> 00:40:25.100
to that song, and it's still beautiful,

00:40:25.100 --> 00:40:27.290
and it's like a through composed thing

00:40:27.290 --> 00:40:28.160
that has a journey to it.

00:40:28.160 --> 00:40:29.210
So I feel like "Lush Life"

00:40:29.210 --> 00:40:31.080
is always a song that I come back to.

00:40:31.080 --> 00:40:34.353
- Hmm, I'm gonna have to check that out.

00:40:34.353 --> 00:40:36.570
(voices muffling)

00:40:36.570 --> 00:40:40.387
Okay, so, "What mood is the
hardest to compose music for

00:40:40.387 --> 00:40:41.220
"and why?"

00:40:42.740 --> 00:40:45.210
- You know, I think the
toughest mood for me is

00:40:47.570 --> 00:40:49.480
comedy or levity.

00:40:49.480 --> 00:40:51.090
- Mmm, yeah.
- I find that

00:40:51.090 --> 00:40:54.760
it's easy to be on the nose, in a way,

00:40:54.760 --> 00:40:57.440
where it's not very interesting
or it feels very expected,

00:40:57.440 --> 00:41:00.460
and how to make something feel funny,

00:41:00.460 --> 00:41:04.980
but feel unique to
whatever that is is funny,

00:41:04.980 --> 00:41:08.790
I feel like is something
that I always find difficult.

00:41:08.790 --> 00:41:11.350
'Cause it's easy to
just kind of go through,

00:41:11.350 --> 00:41:13.000
pull out the pizzicato strings

00:41:13.000 --> 00:41:15.110
and whatever else might
make it (voice muffling).

00:41:15.110 --> 00:41:17.030
This is the funny sounding music,

00:41:17.030 --> 00:41:20.800
but to do it in a way that
makes it feel unique to that,

00:41:20.800 --> 00:41:21.730
I think, is always something

00:41:21.730 --> 00:41:23.810
that's a bit more of a challenge,

00:41:23.810 --> 00:41:25.890
and isn't as immediate to me or for me

00:41:25.890 --> 00:41:28.320
as a lot of other emotions.

00:41:28.320 --> 00:41:29.390
- Yeah, I agree.

00:41:29.390 --> 00:41:32.190
I did have fun with "Just Look Up."

00:41:32.190 --> 00:41:33.698
That was a-

00:41:33.698 --> 00:41:34.531
(voices muffling)

00:41:34.531 --> 00:41:36.510
I think it's the world.

00:41:36.510 --> 00:41:38.920
The world was falling apart,
and I'm like, oh, okay.

00:41:38.920 --> 00:41:41.233
The inspiration wasn't too far.

00:41:42.152 --> 00:41:43.872
Turn the TV on, read the newspaper.

00:41:43.872 --> 00:41:45.066
- Yeah, yeah.

00:41:45.066 --> 00:41:48.787
- So "what would you consider
the most challenging aspect

00:41:48.787 --> 00:41:50.417
"of composing music?"

00:41:52.000 --> 00:41:53.900
- I think it, for me, it's myself,

00:41:53.900 --> 00:41:57.120
getting out of my own
way and the fact that

00:41:57.120 --> 00:41:58.540
the whole time I'm writing music,

00:41:58.540 --> 00:42:00.450
I'm thinking about how bad it is

00:42:00.450 --> 00:42:02.440
or why I shouldn't be doing what I'm doing

00:42:02.440 --> 00:42:04.560
or why I'm making a mistake or,

00:42:04.560 --> 00:42:09.560
I think the stress of the
process, for me, most of the time,

00:42:09.690 --> 00:42:12.410
comes from the internal
dialogue I'm having with myself

00:42:12.410 --> 00:42:13.900
or my fears of,

00:42:13.900 --> 00:42:16.700
you're writing this music for
somebody else to say is good.

00:42:16.700 --> 00:42:19.770
So then it's tough to think about

00:42:20.850 --> 00:42:22.260
whether or not what you're doing

00:42:22.260 --> 00:42:24.270
is going to please this other person.

00:42:24.270 --> 00:42:25.620
You can't really control that.

00:42:25.620 --> 00:42:28.050
And that's why it's always
great to have directors

00:42:28.050 --> 00:42:30.060
that create a safe
environment where you can feel

00:42:30.060 --> 00:42:32.090
not so concerned about that.

00:42:32.090 --> 00:42:34.170
But I do find that that
part of the process

00:42:34.170 --> 00:42:38.570
does create this cycle for
me with internal dialogue,

00:42:38.570 --> 00:42:42.090
that can be a little tough sometimes.

00:42:42.090 --> 00:42:43.420
I feel like that's
probably the hardest part,

00:42:43.420 --> 00:42:46.897
is just getting outta my own way.

00:42:46.897 --> 00:42:47.870
- Yeah.

00:42:47.870 --> 00:42:49.840
Yeah, move, Kris,

00:42:49.840 --> 00:42:53.170
get out the way.
(both laughing)

00:42:53.170 --> 00:42:55.503
Just sing that little song.

00:42:55.503 --> 00:42:57.192
(voices muffling)

00:42:57.192 --> 00:42:59.880
(both laughing)

00:42:59.880 --> 00:43:03.047
So, "Describe a time when
a musician or director

00:43:03.047 --> 00:43:05.767
"disagreed with you, and what happened?"

00:43:06.877 --> 00:43:07.731
That's what they said.

00:43:07.731 --> 00:43:09.313
They want to know what happened.

00:43:09.313 --> 00:43:10.340
- Yeah, what happened?

00:43:10.340 --> 00:43:13.328
Yeah, sure, I mean, it
happens most of the time.

00:43:13.328 --> 00:43:15.210
I think that it...

00:43:15.210 --> 00:43:16.043
Not most of the time.

00:43:16.043 --> 00:43:16.876
Actually, that's not true.

00:43:16.876 --> 00:43:18.920
I think it happens quite frequently.

00:43:18.920 --> 00:43:21.610
And I feel like there might be,

00:43:21.610 --> 00:43:25.300
maybe one time on every project
where that might happen,

00:43:25.300 --> 00:43:28.010
where I feel like I should
go in a certain direction

00:43:28.010 --> 00:43:29.250
and the director doesn't feel that way.

00:43:29.250 --> 00:43:32.910
But most of the time
when I take their advice,

00:43:32.910 --> 00:43:34.735
that ended up being the right way to go.

00:43:34.735 --> 00:43:36.890
One very specific example

00:43:36.890 --> 00:43:38.060
that I always find really interesting

00:43:38.060 --> 00:43:43.060
is the very first score
that I wrote was for,

00:43:44.094 --> 00:43:45.990
excuse me, it was a
documentary for this woman,

00:43:45.990 --> 00:43:49.890
Elaine Stritch, who
was a Broadway actress,

00:43:49.890 --> 00:43:53.480
and she, at the time, she's passed now,

00:43:53.480 --> 00:43:54.670
but at the time she was still alive,

00:43:54.670 --> 00:43:57.360
and she was dealing with diabetes

00:43:57.360 --> 00:43:59.300
and had health issues very frequently,

00:43:59.300 --> 00:44:01.030
and she was in and out of the hospital.

00:44:01.030 --> 00:44:03.430
And there was a theme in the documentary

00:44:03.430 --> 00:44:06.050
where the doctor was telling her,

00:44:06.050 --> 00:44:07.990
she had a really bad health scare,

00:44:07.990 --> 00:44:08.960
and the doctor was telling her

00:44:08.960 --> 00:44:11.000
how important it was for
her to take care of herself

00:44:11.000 --> 00:44:14.374
because of how close she
was to death, essentially.

00:44:14.374 --> 00:44:18.320
And I wrote this piece
of music that felt like

00:44:18.320 --> 00:44:19.800
emotional and felt like it was,

00:44:19.800 --> 00:44:22.280
carried that level of-

00:44:22.280 --> 00:44:24.770
- Of cautionary.
- Yeah, cautioned, yeah.

00:44:24.770 --> 00:44:26.374
And the director was like,

00:44:26.374 --> 00:44:27.910
this feels too sad.

00:44:27.910 --> 00:44:30.130
This feels like she did die.

00:44:30.130 --> 00:44:32.388
Like this feels like it's gone
too far in that direction.

00:44:32.388 --> 00:44:35.377
And I wasn't really sure how to take that.

00:44:35.377 --> 00:44:36.977
And so I tried a couple other things,

00:44:36.977 --> 00:44:38.300
and then it felt like we went too far

00:44:38.300 --> 00:44:39.133
in the other direction,

00:44:39.133 --> 00:44:41.160
where now it didn't feel like tense enough

00:44:41.160 --> 00:44:43.370
or didn't feel like had enough caution.

00:44:43.370 --> 00:44:46.970
And the solution actually ended up being

00:44:46.970 --> 00:44:49.700
changing the main instrument from piano

00:44:49.700 --> 00:44:52.470
to a different instrument,

00:44:52.470 --> 00:44:54.370
because it was the sound of piano

00:44:54.370 --> 00:44:58.210
that made this moment feel
a bit, too, sensitive,

00:44:58.210 --> 00:45:00.750
or too, emotional.

00:45:00.750 --> 00:45:03.040
And so I feel like that was a moment

00:45:03.040 --> 00:45:05.850
I feel really thankful to have
had so early in my career,

00:45:05.850 --> 00:45:07.940
because that immediately
made me feel like,

00:45:07.940 --> 00:45:10.560
oh, this instrument feels a certain way,

00:45:10.560 --> 00:45:11.810
especially for this director.

00:45:11.810 --> 00:45:13.340
And so now I know that

00:45:13.340 --> 00:45:14.980
if somebody says something's too sad,

00:45:14.980 --> 00:45:16.010
it might not be the piece of music.

00:45:16.010 --> 00:45:18.290
It might just be this instrument.

00:45:18.290 --> 00:45:19.807
- Yeah, that's a good note.

00:45:19.807 --> 00:45:23.087
"Can you explain how you might
compose a new piece of work?"

00:45:24.100 --> 00:45:25.640
- Yeah, I think-

00:45:25.640 --> 00:45:27.240
- So do you usually sit down to a piano?

00:45:27.240 --> 00:45:29.230
Or I know, I mean, you're a pianist,

00:45:29.230 --> 00:45:32.430
but sometimes you pick up the guitar or...

00:45:32.430 --> 00:45:34.800
- No, I definitely don't pick
up any other instruments.

00:45:34.800 --> 00:45:36.760
I wish I could play another instrument.

00:45:36.760 --> 00:45:39.213
Like me playing guitar
is a very sad sight.

00:45:41.293 --> 00:45:44.510
it's like piano and
other various keyboards.

00:45:44.510 --> 00:45:47.830
But other than that process
I was mentioning earlier

00:45:47.830 --> 00:45:50.023
about improvising and (voice muffling)

00:45:50.023 --> 00:45:51.350
trying to improvise around feeling,

00:45:51.350 --> 00:45:56.350
oftentimes it's stepping away
from an instrument completely,

00:45:56.930 --> 00:45:59.820
and just trying to think
about what that feels like

00:45:59.820 --> 00:46:02.550
and honestly kind of singing to myself

00:46:02.550 --> 00:46:04.620
or thinking about sounds in my own head

00:46:04.620 --> 00:46:07.600
where I feel like, okay,
if I felt this way,

00:46:07.600 --> 00:46:09.270
what does that sound like?

00:46:09.270 --> 00:46:11.770
And oftentimes I also start thinking about

00:46:11.770 --> 00:46:14.263
what other pieces of music
make me feel that way.

00:46:15.150 --> 00:46:16.720
So, interesting for me to look at,

00:46:16.720 --> 00:46:18.340
there are a couple of pieces

00:46:18.340 --> 00:46:23.340
I know that directly influenced
John Williams, for example,

00:46:24.190 --> 00:46:26.860
with like the sound of
this Shostakovich piece

00:46:26.860 --> 00:46:29.030
or the sound of this other classical piece

00:46:29.030 --> 00:46:30.439
and the emotion that it carried

00:46:30.439 --> 00:46:32.830
and then how he was able to

00:46:32.830 --> 00:46:34.740
totally do his own thing with that.

00:46:34.740 --> 00:46:38.320
And so for me I feel like
I've always looked at that

00:46:38.320 --> 00:46:40.140
as a piece of reference where,

00:46:40.140 --> 00:46:42.820
if I'm writing something
that's supposed to feel angry,

00:46:42.820 --> 00:46:46.540
for example, then oftentimes
if I'm stuck on where to start,

00:46:46.540 --> 00:46:47.830
I'll also think about

00:46:47.830 --> 00:46:51.210
what other music do I
feel makes me feel angry

00:46:51.210 --> 00:46:52.660
or when I'm angry I wanna listen to,

00:46:52.660 --> 00:46:54.810
'cause it makes me feel like
I can tap into that emotion

00:46:54.810 --> 00:46:56.570
or whatever that is.

00:46:56.570 --> 00:46:58.060
And then I go listen to that music

00:46:58.060 --> 00:46:59.420
and see if there's something about that,

00:46:59.420 --> 00:47:01.610
whether it's the tempo or something

00:47:01.610 --> 00:47:04.890
with the harmonies or the
melody or something about that

00:47:04.890 --> 00:47:08.630
that is getting at that
feeling in a very acute way.

00:47:08.630 --> 00:47:10.800
And then I maybe try to
borrow that element of it

00:47:10.800 --> 00:47:11.780
and then build on that.

00:47:11.780 --> 00:47:15.200
So sometimes, like, I love this,

00:47:15.200 --> 00:47:17.660
that video it's on YouTube and Vimeo,

00:47:17.660 --> 00:47:19.290
like "Everything's a Remix,"

00:47:19.290 --> 00:47:23.400
just because it talks
about how every new idea

00:47:23.400 --> 00:47:24.540
is not a new idea.

00:47:24.540 --> 00:47:26.110
That's all built on something else.

00:47:26.110 --> 00:47:29.610
And so not being afraid
of looking elsewhere

00:47:29.610 --> 00:47:31.103
for inspiration because,

00:47:33.390 --> 00:47:35.370
thinking that you have to
reinvent the wheel every time

00:47:35.370 --> 00:47:37.360
is where you get really stuck.

00:47:37.360 --> 00:47:39.431
- Yeah, I have to look at that, too.

00:47:39.431 --> 00:47:40.410
"Everything's a Remix."

00:47:40.410 --> 00:47:41.570
I'm not familiar.

00:47:41.570 --> 00:47:45.290
- It's dope, they literally
like do like AB images of

00:47:46.947 --> 00:47:49.810
"Star Wars" Akira Kurosawa films,

00:47:49.810 --> 00:47:53.770
or like "Star Wars" and
old fighter jet films,

00:47:53.770 --> 00:47:56.970
or Quentin Tarantino and westerns,

00:47:56.970 --> 00:47:58.390
it just kind of shows how much people

00:47:58.390 --> 00:48:01.060
literally take frames from other movies

00:48:01.060 --> 00:48:02.810
as far as the way they shoot something

00:48:02.810 --> 00:48:03.909
or the way they-
- Oh, yeah.

00:48:03.909 --> 00:48:05.200
- And all that, yeah.

00:48:05.200 --> 00:48:06.950
So it's-
- Very cool.

00:48:06.950 --> 00:48:08.927
Okay, last question,

00:48:08.927 --> 00:48:12.667
"Your composition is poorly
received and has to be revised.

00:48:12.667 --> 00:48:14.510
"What do you do?"

00:48:14.510 --> 00:48:16.310
- Well, I feel like that was traumatic.

00:48:16.310 --> 00:48:18.687
I was like, wait, which
composition was poorly received?

00:48:18.687 --> 00:48:20.631
I was like...
(both laughing)

00:48:20.631 --> 00:48:23.464
(voice muffling)

00:48:24.867 --> 00:48:26.260
I feel like, for me,

00:48:26.260 --> 00:48:27.610
I always try to get to,

00:48:27.610 --> 00:48:30.537
immediately to the solution,

00:48:30.537 --> 00:48:32.560
like the answer, like why
was it feeling that way?

00:48:32.560 --> 00:48:34.790
'Cause there's always...

00:48:36.620 --> 00:48:41.420
I don't really sit too long
in the disappointment of that.

00:48:41.420 --> 00:48:42.843
I think that honestly,

00:48:42.843 --> 00:48:47.140
my trouble with sitting in
some sort of doubtful place

00:48:47.140 --> 00:48:49.440
happens before I even
share a piece of music,

00:48:49.440 --> 00:48:52.113
but once I share something
and somebody tells me

00:48:52.113 --> 00:48:53.483
that this didn't work,

00:48:54.470 --> 00:48:56.120
often, I'm like,

00:48:56.120 --> 00:48:58.470
like why didn't that work?

00:48:58.470 --> 00:49:01.070
So I can figure out
what to do differently,

00:49:01.070 --> 00:49:02.780
and asking those questions.

00:49:02.780 --> 00:49:04.667
'Cause oftentimes it's like,

00:49:04.667 --> 00:49:08.030
is it something about the
emotion that I'm missing?

00:49:08.030 --> 00:49:10.187
Is it something about the pace of it?

00:49:10.187 --> 00:49:11.460
Is it something about the instrumentation?

00:49:11.460 --> 00:49:12.640
Is it like...

00:49:12.640 --> 00:49:15.500
Oftentimes I'll ask a director,
if I'm really far off,

00:49:15.500 --> 00:49:16.810
if there's another piece of music

00:49:16.810 --> 00:49:19.330
that does get to that feeling for them

00:49:19.330 --> 00:49:20.880
or that they can think of,

00:49:20.880 --> 00:49:23.110
and it's been really interesting sometimes

00:49:23.110 --> 00:49:24.423
where I'll ask a director that,

00:49:24.423 --> 00:49:26.650
and they'll gimme a reference
that I'll listen to,

00:49:26.650 --> 00:49:29.110
and I'm like, this doesn't
sound anything like

00:49:29.110 --> 00:49:30.840
what we're working on,

00:49:30.840 --> 00:49:32.163
or even sometimes,

00:49:32.163 --> 00:49:34.607
there's sometimes a director is like,

00:49:34.607 --> 00:49:36.796
"I think of the sound of this other piece,

00:49:36.796 --> 00:49:39.520
"and because it has this instrumentation,"

00:49:39.520 --> 00:49:40.353
and I listen to that piece,

00:49:40.353 --> 00:49:42.940
and it doesn't have that
instrumentation at all.

00:49:42.940 --> 00:49:45.080
But oftentimes, I'm like, okay,

00:49:45.080 --> 00:49:46.510
well there's something about this

00:49:46.510 --> 00:49:48.230
that made you feel a certain way.

00:49:48.230 --> 00:49:49.557
And so let me look at that,

00:49:49.557 --> 00:49:51.170
and is there anything about this

00:49:51.170 --> 00:49:52.770
that I can borrow or figure out?

00:49:52.770 --> 00:49:55.130
And so usually it's just asking questions,

00:49:55.130 --> 00:49:56.850
like asking so many questions

00:49:56.850 --> 00:50:00.973
so I can use this mistake or use this,

00:50:03.530 --> 00:50:04.577
yeah, mistake-
- Lesson.

00:50:04.577 --> 00:50:06.100
- Yeah, exactly, lesson.

00:50:06.100 --> 00:50:08.500
So I can kinda figure out
how to do something different

00:50:08.500 --> 00:50:09.920
and move in the right direction.

00:50:09.920 --> 00:50:11.260
- Yeah, absolutely.

00:50:11.260 --> 00:50:14.030
So any final advice for students

00:50:14.030 --> 00:50:16.430
getting into film composing?

00:50:16.430 --> 00:50:18.580
- Yeah, a few things.

00:50:18.580 --> 00:50:19.900
I think,

00:50:19.900 --> 00:50:24.450
one, always be a student
and be obsessed about craft

00:50:24.450 --> 00:50:26.410
and getting better and learning.

00:50:26.410 --> 00:50:28.240
And that's all aspects of this job,

00:50:28.240 --> 00:50:30.900
because it's fascinating
how much of this job

00:50:30.900 --> 00:50:34.290
as a film composer can be
in the technical aspect

00:50:34.290 --> 00:50:38.580
of knowing how to use
certain pieces of software

00:50:38.580 --> 00:50:40.980
or knowing how to
actually record the music

00:50:40.980 --> 00:50:42.530
that you're gonna need to record

00:50:42.530 --> 00:50:46.410
or make that music in terms
of the technical part of that.

00:50:46.410 --> 00:50:50.720
But also, knowing how to express yourself

00:50:50.720 --> 00:50:52.050
with technical ability,

00:50:52.050 --> 00:50:53.730
I think is incredibly important,

00:50:53.730 --> 00:50:55.480
because technique only allows you

00:50:55.480 --> 00:50:58.720
to then express yourself
in a more fluid way.

00:50:58.720 --> 00:51:00.150
And then the second thing to that

00:51:00.150 --> 00:51:05.150
is to really tap into
what it is that you love,

00:51:05.650 --> 00:51:07.520
because that's what makes you unique.

00:51:07.520 --> 00:51:09.810
Like it's so interesting to me how much,

00:51:09.810 --> 00:51:11.700
I'll never forget, some of the albums

00:51:11.700 --> 00:51:14.690
that I found or feel like I found

00:51:14.690 --> 00:51:17.370
and discovered that changed my life.

00:51:17.370 --> 00:51:18.870
And then I talked to
somebody else about it,

00:51:18.870 --> 00:51:19.730
and they're like, "Oh yeah."

00:51:19.730 --> 00:51:21.820
And I'm like, "Wait, how are you not..."

00:51:21.820 --> 00:51:22.653
- Exactly.

00:51:22.653 --> 00:51:23.887
- You know?

00:51:23.887 --> 00:51:26.360
And that kind of taught me that

00:51:26.360 --> 00:51:30.010
the fact that that speaks to
me so deeply, so personally,

00:51:30.010 --> 00:51:31.960
means that there's
something in there for me

00:51:31.960 --> 00:51:33.350
to continue to discover,

00:51:33.350 --> 00:51:35.680
figure out why that's a part of me

00:51:35.680 --> 00:51:37.490
that I'm hearing in
that piece, essentially.

00:51:37.490 --> 00:51:40.500
And so whenever you
recognize those moments,

00:51:40.500 --> 00:51:41.333
really hold onto those things

00:51:41.333 --> 00:51:43.533
and then go deeper into those things.

00:51:44.647 --> 00:51:46.350
Yeah, I feel like those
two pieces of advice

00:51:46.350 --> 00:51:49.560
feel like things that I always
think about myself, even.

00:51:49.560 --> 00:51:51.310
- Yeah, great advice.

00:51:51.310 --> 00:51:55.150
I just wanted to talk a moment
about education being key.

00:51:55.150 --> 00:51:57.860
'Cause I know that not
everybody has access,

00:51:57.860 --> 00:52:01.540
and when I was growing
up, I just didn't want...

00:52:01.540 --> 00:52:04.610
Just for transparency, my
mom was a single mother,

00:52:04.610 --> 00:52:07.600
and her best...

00:52:07.600 --> 00:52:10.260
What she wanted to do for me
was to make sure I survived

00:52:10.260 --> 00:52:11.700
East Oakland, California.

00:52:11.700 --> 00:52:14.560
And so now looking back,

00:52:14.560 --> 00:52:15.930
I have all this information,

00:52:15.930 --> 00:52:18.270
and whenever I have an
opportunity or an audience

00:52:18.270 --> 00:52:20.880
I like to talk directly about education

00:52:20.880 --> 00:52:23.800
and how we have the
ticket to that freedom,

00:52:23.800 --> 00:52:26.423
whatever we want, when we have,

00:52:26.423 --> 00:52:28.700
we do well at school.

00:52:28.700 --> 00:52:30.200
Can you speak to that?

00:52:30.200 --> 00:52:31.170
- Yeah, 100%.

00:52:31.170 --> 00:52:36.170
I feel like it's really
interesting how much,

00:52:36.320 --> 00:52:39.080
the way that our systems have been built,

00:52:39.080 --> 00:52:41.410
especially educational
systems have been built,

00:52:41.410 --> 00:52:45.560
to make us feel like
if you're not excelling

00:52:45.560 --> 00:52:49.060
that you're dumb or you're
not good at these things,

00:52:49.060 --> 00:52:52.820
and in a way where it could
be very easy to just give up

00:52:52.820 --> 00:52:57.383
because you kind of
accept this prescription

00:52:58.640 --> 00:53:00.610
that the system has kind of given you,

00:53:00.610 --> 00:53:03.510
even though a lot of these
systems have been built

00:53:03.510 --> 00:53:06.810
to allow that failure,

00:53:06.810 --> 00:53:08.480
and I feel like,

00:53:08.480 --> 00:53:12.170
really it's all about being curious,

00:53:12.170 --> 00:53:15.070
continuing to move at your own pace

00:53:15.070 --> 00:53:18.473
and learn things at however
you wanna learn them.

00:53:19.410 --> 00:53:22.323
And like you said, knowing that those,

00:53:23.310 --> 00:53:24.350
the more you do that,

00:53:24.350 --> 00:53:28.374
the more you're going to
unlock aspects of your future.

00:53:28.374 --> 00:53:30.110
- [Taura] Absolutely.

00:53:30.110 --> 00:53:32.690
- It's pretty fascinating to see how much

00:53:32.690 --> 00:53:34.240
the world can change for you,

00:53:34.240 --> 00:53:37.670
the more that you feel educated

00:53:37.670 --> 00:53:40.040
in whatever space you
might wanna be focused in

00:53:40.040 --> 00:53:40.943
or any of that.

00:53:41.830 --> 00:53:43.880
And yeah, I feel like

00:53:45.150 --> 00:53:46.000
knowing that

00:53:47.330 --> 00:53:49.680
anything that you're
maybe feeling is difficult

00:53:50.845 --> 00:53:52.195
and that you're failing in,

00:53:53.100 --> 00:53:54.370
you're not a failure.

00:53:54.370 --> 00:53:56.120
It's just something that-

00:53:56.120 --> 00:53:57.730
- A lesson.

00:53:57.730 --> 00:53:59.980
- Exactly, it's something
to continue to chip away at

00:53:59.980 --> 00:54:02.580
and find the people and the environment

00:54:02.580 --> 00:54:03.910
that's gonna help support that.

00:54:03.910 --> 00:54:06.570
I think it's always
disappointing in spaces where

00:54:07.841 --> 00:54:11.340
the environment that you're
in is reinforcing these ideas

00:54:11.340 --> 00:54:13.000
that you might not achieve something.

00:54:13.000 --> 00:54:14.460
And that's just not true.

00:54:14.460 --> 00:54:16.560
Whenever I look at any great person

00:54:16.560 --> 00:54:18.550
that I've either had
the fortune to be around

00:54:18.550 --> 00:54:20.130
or have studied,

00:54:20.130 --> 00:54:21.940
there's a book actually
that I read recently

00:54:21.940 --> 00:54:23.840
about greatness, where it's a myth

00:54:23.840 --> 00:54:27.410
that people are born great or
born with these crazy talents

00:54:27.410 --> 00:54:29.620
or born with these
abilities that every person

00:54:29.620 --> 00:54:32.090
just spent much more
time than anybody else

00:54:32.090 --> 00:54:33.280
doing that thing.

00:54:33.280 --> 00:54:34.113
- Absolutely.

00:54:34.113 --> 00:54:37.443
- If you just spend time in any space

00:54:37.443 --> 00:54:40.481
that you wanna be great at,
then you're going to be great.

00:54:40.481 --> 00:54:41.553
And just keep-
(voices muffling)

00:54:41.553 --> 00:54:42.550
- I totally agree.

00:54:42.550 --> 00:54:43.870
Knowledge is power.

00:54:43.870 --> 00:54:45.712
- Yeah.
- It truly is.

00:54:45.712 --> 00:54:49.850
It's our way up, our way
out, our way through.

00:54:49.850 --> 00:54:51.940
- Yeah, totally, totally agree.

00:54:51.940 --> 00:54:55.330
- Well, Kris, this was amazing.

00:54:55.330 --> 00:54:56.410
I love...

00:54:56.410 --> 00:54:58.822
I could have done this
a couple of more hours,

00:54:58.822 --> 00:55:00.950
just amazing talking to you,

00:55:00.950 --> 00:55:03.190
and I hope to talk to you more.

00:55:03.190 --> 00:55:04.252
- Yeah, definitely.

00:55:04.252 --> 00:55:05.340
Hope to work with you-
- Thank you so much.

00:55:05.340 --> 00:55:06.330
- Yeah, for sure.

00:55:06.330 --> 00:55:08.660
- Thank you, Kris, for that
wonderful conversation.

00:55:08.660 --> 00:55:10.470
And thank you for participating

00:55:10.470 --> 00:55:13.200
in the Academy's Visiting
Artist Film Series,

00:55:13.200 --> 00:55:15.860
FilmAid Edition for Film Composers,

00:55:15.860 --> 00:55:16.693
and songwriters.

